COURSE_DATA["sen418"]={topics:[
{id:'t1',title:'Introduction to IoT and edge computing',why:'Definitions, characteristics and edge vs cloud: scenario questions build on these.',
cards:[
{h:'IoT',b:`<div class="def"><b>Internet of Things</b>A network of physical objects ("things") with sensors, actuators, software and connectivity that collect and exchange data over the internet.</div><p>Characteristics: dynamic and self-adapting · self-configuring · interoperable protocols · unique identity (IP address) · integrated into information networks.</p><p>Components: things (sensors/actuators) · gateway · network · cloud/server · application/UI.</p>`},
{h:'Edge vs fog vs cloud',b:`<table><tr><th>Layer</th><th>Where</th><th>Good for</th></tr><tr><td><b>Edge</b></td><td>On or near the device</td><td>Low latency, privacy, low bandwidth, works offline</td></tr><tr><td><b>Fog</b></td><td>Local gateways/nodes between edge and cloud</td><td>Aggregation, regional processing</td></tr><tr><td><b>Cloud</b></td><td>Remote data centres</td><td>Heavy analytics, storage, ML training</td></tr></table><div class="hook">Decide fast at the edge; learn slowly in the cloud.</div>`},
{h:'The Human Body Analogy & The IoT–Edge–Cloud Trifecta',b:`<p>Lecture 1 anchors the entire course with the <b>Human Body vs Computing Systems</b> analogy (touching a hot object):</p><table><tr><th>Human Body Part</th><th>Role</th><th>Computing Equivalent</th><th>Trifecta Slogan</th></tr><tr><td><b>Eyes / Skin</b></td><td>Detects physical stimuli</td><td><b>Sensors</b> (IoT perception)</td><td><b>Connect</b> (IoT connects the physical world)</td></tr><tr><td><b>Nervous System</b></td><td>Carries information</td><td><b>Network &amp; Gateways</b></td><td>Bridges local devices to edge/cloud</td></tr><tr><td><b>Local Reflexes</b></td><td>Immediate local reaction (jerks hand away)</td><td><b>Edge Computing</b></td><td><b>Respond</b> (processes close to the physical world)</td></tr><tr><td><b>Central Brain</b></td><td>Complex thought &amp; long-term memory</td><td><b>Cloud Infrastructure</b></td><td><b>Scale</b> (large-scale compute, storage, analytics)</td></tr></table><p><b>Slide formula:</b> <code>Physical things + Sensors + Connectivity + Data + Action = IoT</code></p>`},
{h:'What Makes Something IoT? & The 5 Key Characteristics',b:`<p><b>Traditional Thermometer vs IoT Temperature Sensor (Slide 8):</b></p><ul><li><b>Traditional:</b> <code>Temperature &rarr; Display &rarr; Human reads it</code></li><li><b>IoT Sensor:</b> <code>Temperature &rarr; Sensor &rarr; Processing &rarr; Network &rarr; Edge/Cloud &rarr; Application &rarr; Action</code> (collects, transmits, processes, triggers actions, and talks to other systems automatically).</li></ul><p><b>Five Key Characteristics of IoT (Slide 9):</b></p><ol><li><b>Interconnectivity:</b> Devices communicate with one another and with other systems over networks.</li><li><b>Dynamic Nature:</b> Device states (sleeping/awake, connected/disconnected) and environments change continuously.</li><li><b>Scale:</b> Massive numbers of devices (thousands to millions), creating device-management and data-processing challenges.</li><li><b>Heterogeneity:</b> Devices with different hardware, operating systems, protocols, and capabilities interoperate.</li><li><b>Self-Adapting Behaviour:</b> Systems respond automatically to changing conditions (e.g., turning on a fan when temperature exceeds a threshold).</li></ol>`},
{h:'The 4 Ecosystem Layers & 3 Types of Actuators',b:`<p><b>Four Layers of an IoT Ecosystem (Slide 12):</b></p><ol><li><b>Sensors &amp; Actuators:</b> Sensors ("eyes and ears" — temperature, humidity, motion, light, camera, GPS, pressure) detect physical stimuli; Actuators ("muscles" — relays, motors, valves, locks) perform physical actions.</li><li><b>IoT Gateways:</b> Physical device or software acting as a secure bridge between local devices and remote networks/cloud, translating protocols and routing data (e.g., Raspberry Pi, industrial smart router).</li><li><b>Cloud / Server:</b> Centralized historical storage, heavy databases, AI model training, and global analytics.</li><li><b>Applications:</b> User dashboards, mobile apps, automated alert systems, and enterprise decision support.</li></ol><p><b>Three Types of Actuators (Slide 14):</b></p><table><tr><th>Actuator Type</th><th>Energy Source</th><th>Mechanism &amp; Use Case</th></tr><tr><td><b>Electric</b></td><td>Electrical current</td><td>Drives motors/solenoids for precise, clean movement</td></tr><tr><td><b>Hydraulic</b></td><td>Pressurized liquid</td><td>Lifts or moves heavy industrial loads</td></tr><tr><td><b>Pneumatic</b></td><td>Compressed air</td><td>Operates fast-acting industrial valves and switches</td></tr></table>`},
{h:'Cloud Problems (Latency & Bandwidth), Edge Benefits & Privacy Warning',b:`<p><b>Cloud Computing (Slides 18–19):</b> On-demand delivery of compute, storage, databases, networking, and software over the Internet. Advantages: no upfront hardware ownership, rapid vertical/horizontal scaling, high availability. <b>Two limitations for IoT:</b></p><ul><li><b>Latency (Delay):</b> Time between sending data and receiving a response. A self-driving car waiting for <code>Car &rarr; Internet &rarr; Cloud &rarr; Processing &rarr; Internet &rarr; Car &rarr; Brake</code> cannot tolerate multi-second cloud round-trips when milliseconds are critical.</li><li><b>Bandwidth (Pipe Width, measured in bps):</b> Maximum data transfer rate. Thousands of CCTV cameras streaming raw video cause high bandwidth consumption, high costs, network congestion, and unnecessary data transmission.</li></ul><p><b>Three Benefits of Edge Computing (Slides 29–30):</b></p><ol><li><b>Low Latency:</b> <code>Data &rarr; Edge &rarr; Decision</code> locally in milliseconds.</li><li><b>Bandwidth Savings:</b> Filters, summarizes, or compresses locally — e.g., <b><code>Camera produces 100 GB raw data &rarr; Edge &rarr; 2 GB useful information &rarr; Cloud</code></b> (98% reduction!).</li><li><b>Privacy:</b> Keeps sensitive medical, camera, conversation, and location data local. <b style="color:#b91c1c">Slide 30 Warning:</b> <i>"Edge computing does not automatically make a system private or secure. Security and privacy still need to be designed into the system."</i></li></ol>`},
{h:'Debunking the 3 IoT/Edge Architecture Myths (Objective 6)',b:`<p>Lecture 1 Objective 6 highlights three common architecture misconceptions:</p><ol><li><b>Myth 1 — "Everything must go to the cloud":</b> False. Time-critical reflex decisions (autonomous braking, machine safety shutoffs) must execute at the edge to avoid network latency and internet outages.</li><li><b>Myth 2 — "Standalone edge systems replace the cloud completely":</b> False. Edge nodes have limited storage and compute; the cloud is still required for long-term historical archiving, fleet-wide dashboards, and heavy AI model training.</li><li><b>Myth 3 — "All raw sensor data must be transmitted":</b> False. Streaming raw high-frequency telemetry wastes bandwidth; edge gateways filter, aggregate, and transmit only anomalies or compact summaries (e.g., 100 GB raw video &rarr; 2 GB events).</li></ol><div class="trap"><b>Additional context:</b> Fog computing (mentioned in our earlier summary card) is an industry term (Cisco) for intermediate local gateway nodes between edge devices and the cloud; on the lecturer's Lecture 1 slides, local gateways and local servers are grouped directly under <b>Edge Computing</b>.</div>`}],
recall:[['Define IoT.','A network of physical objects with sensors, software and connectivity that collect and exchange data.'],['Four benefits of edge computing.','Low latency, reduced bandwidth, privacy, offline operation.'],['What is fog computing?','An intermediate layer of local nodes/gateways between edge devices and the cloud.'],['Name five IoT system components.','Sensors/actuators, gateway, network, cloud, application.'],['In the Lecture 1 human-body analogy, what do (a) eyes/skin, (b) nervous system, (c) local reflexes, and (d) central brain correspond to?','(a) Eyes/skin = Sensors; (b) Nervous system = Network gateways; (c) Local reflexes = Edge computing (immediate local reaction); (d) Central brain = Cloud infrastructure (complex thought and long-term storage).'],['Write the five-part simple formula for IoT from Lecture 1 Slide 6.','Physical things + Sensors + Connectivity + Data + Action = IoT'],['Name the five key characteristics of IoT listed in Lecture 1 Slide 9.','1) Interconnectivity, 2) Dynamic nature, 3) Scale, 4) Heterogeneity, 5) Self-adapting behaviour.'],['Distinguish between Electric, Hydraulic, and Pneumatic actuators.','Electric actuators use electrical current to drive motors for precise, clean movement; Hydraulic actuators use pressurized liquid to lift or move heavy loads; Pneumatic actuators use compressed air to operate fast-acting industrial valves and switches.'],['Define latency versus bandwidth, and state the Lecture 1 camera data-reduction example for edge bandwidth savings.','Latency is the time delay between sending information and receiving a response. Bandwidth is the maximum rate of data transfer over a network (in bps). On Slide 29, a camera produces 100 GB of raw data, the Edge filters/analyzes it, and sends only 2 GB of useful information to the Cloud.'],['What important caveat does Lecture 1 Slide 30 state about Edge Computing and privacy/security?','Edge computing does NOT automatically make a system private or secure — security and privacy still need to be explicitly designed into the system.']],
quiz:[{k:'mcq',q:'A self-driving car braking decision should run…',o:['In the cloud','At the edge','On a spreadsheet','Weekly'],a:1},{k:'tf',q:'Edge computing increases the bandwidth sent to the cloud.',a:false},{k:'gap',q:'The layer between edge and cloud is ___ computing.',a:'fog'},{q:'In the Lecture 1 human-body analogy, which pair is CORRECT?',o:['Nervous system = Cloud infrastructure; Central brain = Network gateways','Local reflexes = Edge computing; Central brain = Cloud infrastructure','Eyes/skin = Actuators; Local reflexes = Sensors','Central brain = Edge computing; Local reflexes = Cloud infrastructure'],a:1,e:'Eyes/skin are sensors, the nervous system is network gateways, local reflexes are edge computing, and the central brain is cloud infrastructure.',k:'mcq',w:'Eyes/skin are sensors, the nervous system is network gateways, local reflexes are edge computing, and the central brain is cloud infrastructure.'},{q:'A smart home fan automatically turns on whenever the room temperature exceeds 30 °C. Which Lecture 1 IoT characteristic does this directly illustrate?',o:['Heterogeneity','Scale','Self-adapting behaviour','Static configuration'],a:2,e:'Slide 9 defines self-adapting behaviour as responding automatically to changing conditions, using the exact example of turning on a fan when temperature exceeds a threshold.',k:'mcq',w:'Slide 9 defines self-adapting behaviour as responding automatically to changing conditions, using the exact example of turning on a fan when temperature exceeds a threshold.'},{q:'Which type of actuator uses compressed air to operate fast-acting industrial valves and switches?',o:['Hydraulic actuator','Pneumatic actuator','Electric actuator','Piezoelectric sensor'],a:1,e:'Pneumatic actuators use compressed air; hydraulic actuators use pressurized liquid; electric actuators use electrical current.',k:'mcq',w:'Pneumatic actuators use compressed air; hydraulic actuators use pressurized liquid; electric actuators use electrical current.'},{q:'According to Lecture 1 Slide 30, which statement about Edge Computing and privacy is TRUE?',o:['Processing data at the edge automatically guarantees full security and privacy.','Local processing reduces the need to send sensitive raw data to the cloud, but edge computing does NOT automatically make a system private or secure.','Edge computing requires sending 100% of raw medical and camera data to the public cloud first.','Privacy can only be achieved by turning off all local gateways.'],a:1,e:'Slide 30 warns that while local processing reduces raw data transmission, edge computing does not automatically make a system private or secure.',k:'mcq',w:'Slide 30 warns that while local processing reduces raw data transmission, edge computing does not automatically make a system private or secure.'}],
theory:[{q:'A Lagos factory wants machine-failure alerts within 100 ms and monthly trend reports. Recommend an edge/cloud split.',pts:['Background: edge vs cloud','Edge for real-time alerts','Cloud for trends','Fog/gateway aggregation','Scenario','How they combine','Conclusion'],model:`<p><b>Background:</b> edge processes near devices; cloud gives scale. <b>Answer:</b> vibration analysis runs on an edge device for sub-100 ms alerts; summaries go to the cloud for monthly trends and ML. <b>Scenario:</b> unreliable Lagos internet makes edge essential. <b>Combine:</b> a gateway aggregates and buffers data. <b>Conclusion:</b> a hybrid edge–cloud design meets both needs.</p>`},{q:'Using the Lecture 1 Human Body Analogy and the "Connect, Respond, Scale" Trifecta, explain why relying solely on Cloud Computing fails for autonomous vehicles and CCTV networks, and show how combining IoT, Edge, and Cloud resolves these limitations.',model:`<p><b>Background:</b> An IoT system is an ecosystem modelled on the human body: sensors act as the eyes and skin detecting stimuli, network gateways act as the nervous system carrying signals, edge computing acts as local spinal reflexes reacting immediately, and cloud infrastructure acts as the central brain performing deep analysis and long-term memory storage.</p><p><b>Answer and solution:</b></p><ol><li><b>Why Cloud-Only Fails:</b><ul><li><b>The Latency Problem:</b> Latency is the delay between sending data and receiving a response. In a cloud-only path (<code>Car &rarr; Internet &rarr; Cloud &rarr; Processing &rarr; Internet &rarr; Car &rarr; Brake</code>), wide-area network round-trips take tens or hundreds of milliseconds (or fail entirely during connectivity drops), which is fatal when obstacle braking requires millisecond reflexes.</li><li><b>The Bandwidth Problem:</b> Bandwidth is the maximum data transfer rate (in bps) of a network link. Streaming raw video continuously from thousands of CCTV cameras saturates network links, causes congestion, increases cloud ingestion costs, and transmits redundant footage.</li></ul></li><li><b>The IoT + Edge + Cloud Trifecta ("Connect, Respond, Scale"):</b><ul><li><b>IoT (Connect):</b> Sensors and actuators interface directly with the physical world (<code>Physical things + Sensors + Connectivity + Data + Action = IoT</code>).</li><li><b>Edge (Respond):</b> Computing nodes near the source (vehicle computers, Raspberry Pi gateways, intelligent cameras) process telemetry locally for sub-millisecond decisions, filter out noise (e.g., reducing <b>100 GB of raw camera video to 2 GB of useful event data</b>), and keep sensitive raw data local for privacy.</li><li><b>Cloud (Scale):</b> Centralized data centres store historical summaries, train machine-learning models, and serve global dashboards.</li></ul></li></ol><p><b>Scenario:</b> In a smart campus surveillance and shuttle system at PAU, onboard shuttle computers (Edge) process LiDAR and camera feeds locally to brake within 10 ms if a pedestrian steps onto the road, while campus CCTV edge nodes filter 100 GB of daily video down to 2 GB of anomaly clips before uploading to Azure (Cloud) for monthly security trend analytics.</p><p><b>How they combine:</b> Edge computing does not replace the cloud; rather, local gateways bridge the perception layer and the cloud — executing real-time reflex rules locally while forwarding compressed, high-value telemetry upstream when bandwidth allows.</p><p><b>Conclusion:</b> Combining IoT to connect devices, Edge to respond with low latency and 98% bandwidth savings, and Cloud to scale storage and analytics delivers a resilient end-to-end architecture.</p>`,rubric:['Maps the four human-body parts (eyes/skin, nervous system, reflexes, central brain) to Sensors, Gateways, Edge, and Cloud','Explains both the Latency problem (self-driving car braking path) and Bandwidth problem (CCTV raw video congestion)','Cites the three edge benefits (low latency, 100 GB -> 2 GB bandwidth savings, privacy with the security design caveat) and the Connect-Respond-Scale trifecta'],pts:['Maps the four human-body parts (eyes/skin, nervous system, reflexes, central brain) to Sensors, Gateways, Edge, and Cloud','Explains both the Latency problem (self-driving car braking path) and Bandwidth problem (CCTV raw video congestion)','Cites the three edge benefits (low latency, 100 GB -> 2 GB bandwidth savings, privacy with the security design caveat) and the Connect-Respond-Scale trifecta']}]},
{id:'t2',title:'Domain-specific IoT',why:'Examples per domain: easy marks if you can name them.',
cards:[
{h:'Domains',b:`<table><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Examples</th></tr><tr><td>Home</td><td>Smart lighting, appliances, intrusion detection, smoke/gas detectors</td></tr><tr><td>Cities</td><td>Smart parking, lighting, roads, structural health, surveillance</td></tr><tr><td>Environment</td><td>Weather, air and noise pollution, forest fire, flood detection</td></tr><tr><td>Energy</td><td>Smart grids, renewable systems, prognostics</td></tr><tr><td>Retail/logistics</td><td>Inventory, smart payments, fleet tracking, shipment monitoring</td></tr><tr><td>Agriculture</td><td>Smart irrigation, greenhouse control</td></tr><tr><td>Industry</td><td>Machine diagnosis, indoor air quality</td></tr><tr><td>Health</td><td>Wearables, remote patient monitoring</td></tr></table>`},
{h:'Home Automation & Smart Cities: Sensors, Protocols & Slide Facts',b:`<table><tr><th>Application</th><th>Sensors / Technologies on Slides</th><th>How It Works &amp; Key Slide Facts</th></tr><tr><td><b>Smart Lighting (Home)</b></td><td>Solid-state lighting (LEDs), IP-enabled lights, occupancy &amp; temperature sensors</td><td>Adapts spectral/temporal light characteristics (intensity, colour) to ambient conditions and human movement; controllable via mobile/web app.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Smart Appliances</b></td><td><b>RFID tags</b> (inside smart refrigerator), smart thermostat, washer/dryer</td><td>Washer notifies when cycle ends; thermostat learns user preferences; fridge tracks items via RFID and alerts when stock is low.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Intrusion Detection</b></td><td><b>PIR (Passive Infrared)</b> sensors, door sensors, security cameras, <b>UPnP</b></td><td>Sends SMS/email alerts with image grab or short video clip; cloud location-aware services alert occupants, security post, or police; UPnP + image processing recognizes intrusions.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Smoke / Gas Detectors</b></td><td><b>Optical, ionization, or air-sampling</b> (smoke); <b>CO &amp; LPG</b> sensors (gas)</td><td>Smart detectors give human-voice alerts stating where the problem is and send SMS/email to the user and fire safety department.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Smart Parking</b></td><td>Per-slot occupancy sensors + local controller</td><td>Local controller aggregates slot status and updates cloud database; drivers check smartphones, tablets, or in-car navigation, reducing traffic congestion.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Smart Road Lighting</b></td><td>Internet-connected adaptive street lights</td><td>Slide states road lighting accounts for <b>19% of global electricity use and ~6% of global greenhouse gas emissions</b>. Supports custom schedules for foggy days or festivals and light-to-light sensor communication.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Smart Roads &amp; Structural Health</b></td><td>Road condition nodes; bridge/building <b>vibration sensors</b></td><td>Smart roads warn drivers hundreds of metres or dozens of miles ahead. Structural health monitoring analyses vibration levels to detect cracks, locate damage, and calculate <b>remaining life</b> of bridges/buildings.</td></tr></table><div class="trap"><b>Slide Fact Flag:</b> Lecture 2 Slide 9 states <i>"roading lighting is responsible for 19% of global electricity use and around 6% of global greenhouse gas emissions"</i> (in the original IEA report, 19% is for <i>all</i> lighting globally). In the exam, quote the slide's <b>19% / 6%</b> figures while noting the IEA context if asked!</div>`},
{h:'Environment, Retail, Logistics, Agriculture & Health Details',b:`<table><tr><th>Domain &amp; App</th><th>Specific Sensors / Tech</th><th>Slide Mechanism &amp; Outcomes</th></tr><tr><td><b>Environment: Air &amp; Noise Pollution</b></td><td>Gaseous &amp; meteorological sensors (<b>CO<sub>2</sub>, CO, NO, NO<sub>2</sub></b>); city noise sensors</td><td>Air quality stations communicate wirelessly with backend server via <b>M2M communication</b>. Noise data is aggregated into <b>urban noise maps</b> for policy makers (noise causes sleep disruption and stress).</td></tr><tr><td><b>Environment: River Floods &amp; Forest Fires</b></td><td><b>Ultrasonic sensors</b> (water level) + <b>flow velocity sensors</b> (flow rate); forest nodes (temp, humidity, light)</td><td>Flood alerts trigger on rapid water-level and flow-rate rise from continuous rainfall. Forest fire nodes enable early detection (causes: lightning, human negligence, volcanic eruptions, rock-fall sparks).</td></tr><tr><td><b>Retail: Inventory &amp; Smart Payment</b></td><td><b>RFID tags &amp; shelf/warehouse readers</b>; <b>NFC + Bluetooth</b></td><td>RFID tracks stock in real time. In contactless smart payment, <b>NFC (low speed) initiates initial pairing</b> by proximity/touch, while <b>actual data transfer takes place over Bluetooth</b>.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Logistics: Fleet &amp; Shipment Monitoring</b></td><td><b>GPS</b> (fleet); container <b>temperature, pressure, humidity</b> sensors + truck positioning</td><td>Fleet tracking detects route deviations and supply-chain bottlenecks. Shipment monitoring tracks fresh food with a limited time budget and <b>dynamically re-routes trucks to closer destinations</b> before spoilage occurs.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Agriculture &amp; Health</b></td><td>Greenhouse (temp, humidity, soil moisture, light, <b>CO<sub>2</sub></b> + valves &amp; fan switches); Wearables (<b>Google Glass, Moto 360</b>, Android smartwatches)</td><td>Smart irrigation releases pipe water only when soil moisture drops below a threshold. Wearables monitor heart rate, calories burnt, and run mobile OS apps.</td></tr></table>`},
{h:'End-to-End Assignment Architecture Blueprint (Flood, Cold-Chain, Greenhouse)',b:`<p>The PAU CSC418 Coursework Assignment requires designing a 4-layer end-to-end pipeline (<b>Perception &rarr; Edge/Gateway &rarr; Network Protocol &rarr; Cloud Backend/Dashboard</b>) with explicit sensors, actuators, and thresholds:</p><table><tr><th>Scenario</th><th>Sensors &amp; Thresholds</th><th>Edge Action &amp; Actuators</th><th>Network &amp; Cloud Layer</th></tr><tr><td><b>A: River Flood Early Warning</b></td><td>Ultrasonic sensor (water level &gt; 3.5 m critical offset) + flow velocity sensor (&gt; 2.5 m/s rise rate)</td><td>ESP32/STM32 node triggers local siren/strobe beacon &amp; closes floodgate actuator within milliseconds</td><td>LoRaWAN / Cellular + <b>MQTT</b> publish to cloud broker &rarr; SMS/dashboard alert to residents &amp; emergency services</td></tr><tr><td><b>B: Cold-Chain Food Shipment</b></td><td>Container temperature (&gt; 4 °C ceiling for dairy/meat), humidity (&gt; 85%), pressure + GPS</td><td>In-truck edge gateway activates backup compressor relay &amp; sounds cab buzzer immediately</td><td>4G/5G cellular + <b>MQTT/CoAP</b> to cloud &rarr; calculates remaining shelf-life budget &amp; re-routes truck to nearest depot</td></tr><tr><td><b>C: Automated Greenhouse &amp; Irrigation</b></td><td>Soil moisture (&lt; 25% VWC threshold), air temp (&gt; 32 °C), humidity, light, CO<sub>2</sub> (&lt; 400 ppm)</td><td>ESP32 edge controller opens solenoid water valve when moisture &lt; 25% and switches exhaust fans on when temp &gt; 32 °C</td><td>Wi-Fi/LoRa + <b>MQTT</b> to cloud server &rarr; correlates yield productivity with micro-climate control strategies</td></tr></table>`}],
recall:[['Two IoT applications for smart cities.','Smart parking, smart lighting, smart roads, structural health monitoring.'],['Two IoT applications in agriculture.','Smart irrigation, greenhouse control.'],['Two in logistics.','Fleet tracking, shipment monitoring.'],['In Lecture 2 river flood detection, which exact sensor measures water level and which measures flow rate?','Ultrasonic sensors measure water level, and flow velocity sensors measure flow rate.'],['In Lecture 2 smart payment systems, how do NFC and Bluetooth work together when a customer taps their smartphone?','NFC (which operates at very short range and low speeds) initiates the initial pairing of the devices, while the actual higher-speed data transfer takes place over Bluetooth.'],['What are the three smoke-detection techniques and two harmful gases named on Lecture 2 Slide 7?','Smoke detectors use optical detection, ionization, or air-sampling techniques. Gas detectors monitor carbon monoxide (CO) and liquid petroleum gas (LPG).'],['What percentage of global electricity use and greenhouse gas emissions does Lecture 2 Slide 9 attribute to road lighting, and what is the real-world source nuance?','Slide 9 states road lighting accounts for 19% of global electricity use and around 6% of global greenhouse gas emissions (in the underlying IEA report, 19% refers to all lighting globally).'],['What four gases are monitored in Lecture 2 air pollution monitoring, and what technology do the distributed stations use to talk to the backend server?','CO2, CO, NO, and NO2 (using gaseous and meteorological sensors), communicating wirelessly with the backend server via Machine-to-Machine (M2M) communication.'],['What five parameters are sensed inside an automated IoT greenhouse on Lecture 2 Slide 21, and which two actuators control the climate?','Sensed parameters: temperature, humidity, soil moisture, light, and carbon dioxide (CO2) levels. Actuators: valves for releasing water and switches for controlling fans.']],
quiz:[{k:'mcq',q:'Soil-moisture sensors that switch pumps are…',o:['Smart grid','Smart irrigation','Smart parking','Wearables'],a:1},{k:'tf',q:'Structural health monitoring is a smart-city application.',a:true},{q:'In an IoT river flood detection system (Lecture 2 Slide 15), which pair of sensors is used to monitor water level and flow rate respectively?',o:['PIR sensor and RFID reader','Ultrasonic sensor and flow velocity sensor','Barometric pressure sensor and gyroscope','Optical ionization sensor and thermocouple'],a:1,e:'Slide 15 specifies ultrasonic sensors for water level and flow velocity sensors for water flow rate.',k:'mcq',w:'Slide 15 specifies ultrasonic sensors for water level and flow velocity sensors for water flow rate.'},{q:'How do NFC and Bluetooth collaborate in a Lecture 2 smart payment system when a customer taps their phone at a point-of-sale terminal?',o:['Bluetooth initiates pairing at 10 metres, and NFC transfers the bulk data.','NFC (which offers low speeds) initiates the initial pairing by proximity, while the actual data transfer takes place over Bluetooth.','NFC and Bluetooth are mutually exclusive and cannot be used together.','NFC reads RFID tags on the shelves while Bluetooth controls the warehouse doors.'],a:1,e:'Slide 18 states that NFC (low speed) initiates initial pairing of devices to establish a Bluetooth connection while the actual data transfer takes place over Bluetooth.',k:'mcq',w:'Slide 18 states that NFC (low speed) initiates initial pairing of devices to establish a Bluetooth connection while the actual data transfer takes place over Bluetooth.'},{q:'In a smart refrigerator (Lecture 2 Slide 5) and retail inventory management (Slide 17), which technology is attached to products to track stock levels automatically?',o:['PIR (Passive Infrared) tags','RFID (Radio Frequency Identification) tags','Ultrasonic transducers','Pneumatic valves'],a:1,e:'Both smart refrigerators (Slide 5) and retail shelves/warehouses (Slide 17) use RFID tags and RFID readers to track items and alert when stock is low.',k:'mcq',w:'Both smart refrigerators (Slide 5) and retail shelves/warehouses (Slide 17) use RFID tags and RFID readers to track items and alert when stock is low.'},{q:'What is the primary output generated from aggregating city-wide IoT noise pollution sensor data on Lecture 2 Slide 14?',o:['Real-time seismic earthquake alerts','Urban noise maps used by policy makers and urban planners to control noise near residential areas, schools, and parks','Automated traffic light switching schedules','Bridge remaining-life calculations'],a:1,e:'Slide 14 states that aggregated noise sensor data is used to generate urban noise maps to help policy makers protect residential areas, schools, and parks from sleep disruption and stress.',k:'mcq',w:'Slide 14 states that aggregated noise sensor data is used to generate urban noise maps to help policy makers protect residential areas, schools, and parks from sleep disruption and stress.'}],
theory:[{q:'Describe a smart irrigation system for a Nigerian farm: sensors, processing and benefits.',pts:['Sensors (soil moisture, temperature)','Microcontroller/edge logic','Actuators (pump valves)','Connectivity','Cloud dashboard','Benefits'],model:`<p>Soil-moisture and temperature sensors feed an ESP32 that opens valves below a threshold; data goes over LoRa/GSM to a cloud dashboard for the farmer. Benefits: water savings, yield, remote control.</p>`},{q:'Coursework Assignment Scenario: Design an end-to-end IoT architecture for EITHER (a) a Real-Time River Flood Early Warning System OR (b) Cold-Chain Food Shipment Monitoring. Specify the exact sensors, actuators, operational thresholds, 4-layer data pipeline (Perception -> Edge/Gateway -> Network Protocol -> Cloud/Dashboard), and explain how it outperforms manual monitoring.',model:`<p><b>Background:</b> Domain-specific IoT systems transform reactive, error-prone manual inspections into closed-loop, self-adapting infrastructures. Whether protecting urban watercourses from flash floods or preventing perishable food spoilage in transit, every robust deployment follows a four-layer pipeline from physical perception to edge reflex control, network transport, and cloud analytics.</p><p><b>Answer and solution:</b></p><p><b>1. Sensors, Actuators &amp; Operational Thresholds:</b></p><ul><li><b>Option A (River Flood Early Warning):</b><ul><li><b>Sensors:</b> <b>Ultrasonic sensors</b> mounted beneath bridges to measure river water level continuously, paired with <b>flow velocity sensors</b> measuring water speed and rain gauges.</li><li><b>Threshold Parameters:</b> Warning threshold at water level &ge; 2.8 m or flow velocity &ge; 1.8 m/s; critical flood intervention threshold at water level &ge; 3.5 m or rapid rise &gt; 0.5 m within 15 minutes.</li><li><b>Actuators:</b> High-decibel outdoor warning sirens/voice alarms, automated road-barrier gates blocking low-lying bridge approaches, and motorized sluice/floodgate valves.</li></ul></li><li><b>Option B (Cold-Chain Food Shipment Monitoring):</b><ul><li><b>Sensors:</b> Inside the refrigerated container: <b>temperature, humidity, and pressure sensors</b> plus door-open contact switches; on the truck: <b>GPS module</b> for real-time location.</li><li><b>Threshold Parameters:</b> Temperature ceiling &gt; 4.0 °C for chilled produce (or &gt; &minus;15 °C for frozen goods) for more than 10 minutes; relative humidity outside 85%–90%.</li><li><b>Actuators:</b> Secondary refrigeration compressor relay, audible/visual driver cab alarm, and automated vents.</li></ul></li></ul><p><b>2. Four-Layer Architectural Pipeline &amp; Connectivity Protocols:</b></p><ol><li><b>Perception Layer:</b> Sensors sample physical metrics every 10–30 seconds and feed raw electrical/digital readings via I<sup>2</sup>C/UART to the local node.</li><li><b>Edge / Gateway Layer:</b> An <b>ESP32</b> or industrial gateway filters sensor noise, evaluates threshold rules locally in milliseconds, and immediately triggers the local actuator (sounding the flood siren or starting the backup chiller) even if cellular coverage drops.</li><li><b>Network Protocol Layer:</b> Field nodes transmit compact JSON payloads over <b>LoRaWAN</b> or <b>4G/5G cellular</b> links using the lightweight <b>MQTT publish-subscribe</b> protocol (or <b>CoAP over UDP</b>) to a cloud message broker.</li><li><b>Cloud Backend &amp; Application Layer:</b> The cloud ingests telemetry into time-series storage, runs predictive models (calculating flood arrival times or remaining food shelf-life budget to dynamically <b>re-route the food truck to a closer destination</b>), and dispatches SMS/mobile push alerts to authorities, drivers, and distributors.</li></ol><p><b>Scenario:</b> During heavy rainfall in Lagos (or a refrigerator compressor fault on the Sagamu–Benin expressway), manual human inspection would only discover the crisis hours later after floodwaters breach homes or the cargo rots. In our IoT architecture, the edge node reacts within 50 ms of crossing the threshold while MQTT immediately notifies downstream stakeholders.</p><p><b>How they combine:</b> Local edge threshold checks guarantee zero-latency physical protection during internet blackouts, while cloud aggregation correlates multi-node trends (upstream river gauges or fleet traffic bottlenecks) to coordinate city-wide warnings or dynamic supply-chain re-routing.</p><p><b>Conclusion:</b> By uniting specific perception sensors (ultrasonic/flow or temp/humidity/GPS), deterministic edge actuation, lightweight MQTT/LoRaWAN transport, and predictive cloud dashboards, the IoT architecture eliminates human observation delay and prevents catastrophic loss.</p>`,rubric:['Identifies the exact lecture sensors (ultrasonic + flow velocity for floods; temperature + humidity + pressure + GPS for shipment monitoring)','Specifies concrete numerical threshold limits and at least one physical actuator (siren/floodgate or backup chiller relay/cab alarm)','Traces data across all four layers (Perception -> Edge/Gateway -> Network Protocol such as LoRaWAN/Cellular + MQTT/CoAP -> Cloud/Dashboard)','Explains how automated real-time edge actuation and predictive cloud re-routing/early warning outperform manual inspection'],pts:['Identifies the exact lecture sensors (ultrasonic + flow velocity for floods; temperature + humidity + pressure + GPS for shipment monitoring)','Specifies concrete numerical threshold limits and at least one physical actuator (siren/floodgate or backup chiller relay/cab alarm)','Traces data across all four layers (Perception -> Edge/Gateway -> Network Protocol such as LoRaWAN/Cellular + MQTT/CoAP -> Cloud/Dashboard)','Explains how automated real-time edge actuation and predictive cloud re-routing/early warning outperform manual inspection']}]},
{id:'t3',title:'Evolution and architecture: protocols, models, M2M',why:'Protocol layers, communication models and IoT vs M2M.',
cards:[
{h:'Protocols by layer',b:`<table><tr><th>Layer</th><th>Protocols</th></tr><tr><td>Link</td><td>Ethernet 802.3, Wi-Fi 802.11, Zigbee 802.15.4, Bluetooth, 2G/3G/4G</td></tr><tr><td>Network</td><td>IPv4, IPv6, <b>6LoWPAN</b> (IPv6 over low-power networks)</td></tr><tr><td>Transport</td><td>TCP, UDP</td></tr><tr><td>Application</td><td>HTTP, CoAP, WebSocket, <b>MQTT</b>, XMPP, DDS, AMQP</td></tr></table>`},
{h:'Communication models',b:`<p><b>Request–response</b> (HTTP, CoAP) · <b>publish–subscribe</b> (MQTT: publishers send to a broker topic; subscribers receive) · <b>push–pull</b> (queues) · <b>exclusive pair</b> (bidirectional, WebSocket).</p><p>APIs: REST (stateless request–response) vs WebSocket (full-duplex, persistent connection).</p>`},
{h:'IoT vs M2M',b:`<table><tr><th>M2M</th><th>IoT</th></tr><tr><td>Device-to-device, often proprietary, non-IP protocols</td><td>IP-based, interoperable</td></tr><tr><td>Local, hardware focus</td><td>Cloud, software and analytics focus</td></tr></table>`},
{h:'Things in IoT & Link Layer Protocols (802.3, 802.11, 802.16, 802.15.4)',b:`<p><b>Four Interfaces of an IoT Device (Slide 2):</b> (i) I/O interfaces for sensors, (ii) interfaces for Internet connectivity, (iii) memory and storage interfaces, and (iv) audio/video interfaces.</p><p><b>Link Layer Protocols (Slides 4–5):</b> Determine how packets are physically coded and signaled over the local medium (copper wire, coaxial cable, optical fiber, or radio wave):</p><table><tr><th>Standard</th><th>Sub-variants &amp; Frequency Bands</th><th>Data Rates</th></tr><tr><td><b>IEEE 802.3 (Ethernet)</b></td><td><code>802.3</code> (10BASE5 coax), <code>802.3i</code> (10BASE-T copper twisted-pair), <code>802.3j</code> (10BASE-F fiber), <code>802.3ae</code> (10 Gbit/s fiber)</td><td><b>10 Mb/s to 40 Gb/s+</b></td></tr><tr><td><b>IEEE 802.11 (Wi-Fi WLAN)</b></td><td><code>802.11a</code> (5 GHz), <code>802.11b/g</code> (2.4 GHz), <code>802.11n</code> (2.4/5 GHz), <code>802.11ac</code> (5 GHz), <code>802.11ad</code> (60 GHz)</td><td><b>1 Mb/s to 6.75 Gb/s</b></td></tr><tr><td><b>IEEE 802.16 (WiMAX)</b></td><td>Wireless broadband; <code>802.16m</code> delivers 100 Mb/s mobile and 1 Gb/s fixed</td><td><b>1.5 Mb/s to 1 Gb/s</b></td></tr><tr><td><b>IEEE 802.15.4 (LR-WPAN)</b></td><td>Low-Rate Wireless Personal Area Networks; foundation for <b>ZigBee</b> &amp; 6LoWPAN; low-cost, low-power</td><td><b>40 Kb/s to 250 Kb/s</b></td></tr></table>`},
{h:'Cellular Link Layer (2G/3G/4G & 5G NR Service Pillars)',b:`<ul><li><b>2G / 3G / 4G Mobile Communication (Slide 6):</b><ul><li><b>2G:</b> GSM and CDMA</li><li><b>3G:</b> UMTS and CDMA2000</li><li><b>4G:</b> LTE</li><li><b>Data rates:</b> <b>9.6 Kb/s (2G)</b> up to <b>100 Mb/s (4G)</b>.</li></ul></li><li><b>5G New Radio (5G NR — 3GPP Release 15+):</b><ul><li><b>Frequency Ranges:</b> <b>FR1 (Sub-6 GHz: 410 MHz to 7.125 GHz)</b> for wide-area coverage; <b>FR2 (mmWave: 24.25 GHz to 71 GHz)</b> for high-density, ultra-fast transfers.</li><li><b>Performance:</b> Peak data rates from <b>1 Gbps up to 20 Gbps</b> with latency as low as <b>1 ms</b>.</li><li><b>Three 5G IoT Service Pillars:</b><ol><li><b>eMBB (Enhanced Mobile Broadband):</b> High-bandwidth data/video transfers.</li><li><b>URLLC (Ultra-Reliable Low-Latency Communications):</b> Mission-critical ~1 ms control (autonomous vehicles, robotics, remote surgery).</li><li><b>mMTC (Massive Machine-Type Communications):</b> Connecting up to millions of low-power IoT sensors per square kilometre.</li></ol></li></ul></li></ul>`},
{h:'Network Layer (IPv4, IPv6, 6LoWPAN) & Transport Layer (TCP vs UDP)',b:`<p><b>Network / Internet Layer (Slides 8–9):</b> Performs host addressing and packet routing across networks:</p><ul><li><b>IPv4 (RFC 791):</b> 32-bit address scheme = $2^{32} = 4,294,967,296$ addresses; exhausted in <b>2011</b>. Does not guarantee delivery itself.</li><li><b>IPv6 (RFC 2460 on slide; updated by RFC 8200):</b> 128-bit address scheme = $2^{128} \\approx 3.4 \\times 10^{38}$ addresses.</li><li><b>6LoWPAN (IPv6 over Low-power Wireless Personal Area Networks):</b> Operates in the <b>2.4 GHz</b> band at <b>250 Kb/s</b> over <b>IEEE 802.15.4</b> and defines header <b>compression mechanisms</b> so IPv6 datagrams fit tiny low-power devices.</li></ul><p><b>Transport Layer (Slides 10–11):</b> End-to-end message transfer (error control, segmentation, flow control, congestion control):</p><table><tr><th>Feature</th><th>TCP (Transmission Control Protocol)</th><th>UDP (User Datagram Protocol)</th></tr><tr><td>Connection &amp; State</td><td><b>Connection-oriented</b> (handshake) &amp; <b>stateful</b></td><td><b>Connectionless</b> (no setup handshake) &amp; <b>stateless</b> (transaction-oriented)</td></tr><tr><td>Reliability &amp; Ordering</td><td>Guaranteed in-order delivery, duplicate elimination, lost-packet retransmission, flow &amp; congestion control</td><td>No guaranteed delivery, no ordering, no duplicate elimination; minimal overhead</td></tr><tr><td>Used by</td><td>HTTP/HTTPS, WebSocket, MQTT, SMTP, FTP</td><td><b>CoAP</b>, real-time/time-sensitive small IoT payloads</td></tr></table>`},
{h:'Application Layer Protocols: HTTP, CoAP, WebSocket, MQTT, XMPP',b:`<table><tr><th>Protocol</th><th>Transport</th><th>Model / Architecture</th><th>Key Slide Facts</th></tr><tr><td><b>HTTP</b></td><td>TCP (Port 80)</td><td>Request–Response (Client–Server, Stateless)</td><td>Commands: <code>GET, PUT, POST, DELETE, HEAD, TRACE, OPTIONS</code>; uses URIs.</td></tr><tr><td><b>CoAP</b> (Constrained Application Protocol)</td><td><b>UDP</b></td><td>Request–Response (Client–Server, Connectionless datagrams)</td><td>Designed for M2M constrained devices/networks; supports <code>GET, PUT, POST, DELETE</code> and easily interfaces with HTTP.</td></tr><tr><td><b>WebSocket</b></td><td>TCP</td><td><b>Exclusive Pair</b> (Full-duplex over a single persistent socket)</td><td>Starts with an <b>HTTP upgrade handshake</b>; once open, streams messages both ways without per-message setup/teardown overhead.</td></tr><tr><td><b>MQTT</b> (Message Queue Telemetry Transport)</td><td>TCP</td><td><b>Publish–Subscribe</b> (Client–Broker)</td><td>Lightweight for low memory/bandwidth; devices publish to <b>topics</b> on an <b>MQTT Broker</b>, which forwards to subscribed clients.</td></tr><tr><td><b>XMPP</b> (Extensible Messaging &amp; Presence Protocol)</td><td>TCP</td><td>Decentralized Client–Server &amp; Server–Server</td><td>Real-time streaming of small <b>XML chunks</b>; powers presence, messaging, chat, gaming, and real-time device-to-device data.</td></tr></table><div class="trap"><b>Additional context:</b> <b>DDS</b> (Data Distribution Service — real-time device-to-device pub-sub middleware) and <b>AMQP</b> (Advanced Message Queuing Protocol — enterprise point-to-point/pub-sub queuing) appear on the Lecture 3 protocol stack diagram.</div>`},
{h:'Logical Design: 6 Functional Blocks, 4 Comm Models & 5 REST Constraints',b:`<p><b>Six IoT Functional Blocks (Slides 16–17):</b></p><ol><li><b>Device:</b> Sensing, actuation, monitoring, and direct control hardware.</li><li><b>Communication:</b> Data transfer using network standards and protocols.</li><li><b>Services:</b> Device discovery, real-time monitoring, control pipelines, and data publishing utilities.</li><li><b>Management:</b> Governance features to manage, configure, and maintain the IoT infrastructure.</li><li><b>Security:</b> User authentication, access authorization, message validation, and data encryption.</li><li><b>Application:</b> User interface to monitor status, view analytics, and control devices remotely.</li></ol><p><b>Four Communication Models (Slides 18–19):</b></p><ul><li><b>Request–Response:</b> Client sends request, server fetches resource representation and replies; <b>stateless</b> (each pair is independent).</li><li><b>Publish–Subscribe:</b> Publishers send to <b>topics</b> on a <b>broker</b>; consumers subscribe to topics; publishers are unaware of consumers.</li><li><b>Push–Pull:</b> Producers push data to <b>queues</b> and consumers pull from queues; queues decouple them and act as a <b>buffer when push and pull rates mismatch</b>.</li><li><b>Exclusive Pair:</b> Bidirectional, <b>full-duplex</b>, persistent connection; <b>stateful</b> (server tracks all open connections until client closes).</li></ul><p><b>Five REST Architectural Constraints (Slides 20–22):</b> <b>1. Client–Server</b> (separation of UI vs storage concerns) &middot; <b>2. Stateless</b> (every request carries full info; session state kept entirely on client) &middot; <b>3. Cache-able</b> (responses labelled cacheable/non-cacheable for reuse) &middot; <b>4. Layered System</b> (components cannot see beyond the immediate layer; intermediaries can answer) &middot; <b>5. Uniform Interface</b> (resources identified by URIs, separate from representations; self-descriptive messages).</p>`},
{h:'M2M vs IoT & M2M Area Network Protocols',b:`<p><b>Machine-to-Machine (M2M — Slide 26):</b> Networking of machines/devices for remote monitoring, control, and data exchange.</p><ul><li><b>M2M Area Network:</b> Comprises M2M nodes with embedded hardware modules for sensing, actuation, and communication.</li><li><b>M2M Local Protocols:</b> <b>Bluetooth, ModBus, M-Bus, Wireless M-Bus, Power Line Communication (PLC), 6LoWPAN, IEEE 802.15.4</b>.</li><li><b>Key Architectural Difference (Local vs Remote M2M):</b> While <b>M2M area networks</b> typically use <b>proprietary or non-IP based communication protocols</b>, the <b>communication network</b> connecting remote M2M area networks uses wired or wireless <b>IP-based networks</b>.</li></ul>`}],
recall:[['Name the four IoT communication models.','Request–response, publish–subscribe, push–pull, exclusive pair.'],['How does MQTT work?','Publishers send messages to topics on a broker; subscribers to those topics receive them.'],['What is 6LoWPAN?','IPv6 over Low-power Wireless Personal Area Networks.'],['IoT vs M2M in one line.','M2M is device-to-device, often proprietary; IoT is IP-based, interoperable and cloud-centred.'],['Name the four types of interfaces an IoT device may consist of according to Lecture 3 Slide 2.','1) I/O interfaces for sensors, 2) Interfaces for Internet connectivity, 3) Memory and storage interfaces, 4) Audio/video interfaces.'],['State the frequency bands, peak data rates, latency, and three service pillars of 5G NR from Lecture 3 Slide 6.','Frequency ranges: FR1 (Sub-6 GHz: 410 MHz to 7.125 GHz) and FR2 (mmWave: 24.25 GHz to 71 GHz). Peak data rates: 1 Gbps to 20 Gbps; latency as low as 1 ms. Service pillars: eMBB (Enhanced Mobile Broadband), URLLC (Ultra-Reliable Low-Latency Communications), and mMTC (Massive Machine-Type Communications).'],['Compare IPv4 and IPv6 bit lengths, total address counts, RFC numbers, and the year IPv4 exhausted.','IPv4 (RFC 791) uses 32-bit addresses (2^32 = 4,294,967,296 addresses) and was exhausted in 2011. IPv6 (RFC 2460 on slides) uses 128-bit addresses (2^128 = 3.4 x 10^38 addresses).'],['How does CoAP differ from HTTP at the transport layer, and why is it suited for M2M applications?','Like HTTP, CoAP uses a request-response model with GET, PUT, POST, and DELETE methods, but CoAP runs over connectionless UDP datagrams instead of TCP, eliminating TCP handshake overhead for constrained devices and networks.'],['Name the six IoT functional blocks in the logical design of IoT (Lecture 3 Slides 16–17).','1) Device, 2) Communication, 3) Services, 4) Management, 5) Security, 6) Application.'],['What is the difference between the Publish-Subscribe and Push-Pull communication models?','In Publish-Subscribe, publishers send data to topics on a broker, which immediately pushes copies to all subscribed consumers. In Push-Pull, producers push messages into queues and consumers pull them at their own pace, so queues act as a buffer when producer push rates and consumer pull rates differ.'],['List the five REST architectural constraints on Lecture 3 Slides 20–22.','1) Client-Server, 2) Stateless, 3) Cache-able, 4) Layered System, 5) Uniform Interface.'],['Name five M2M area network protocols from Lecture 3 Slide 26, and explain how the M2M area network differs from the M2M communication network.','Protocols: Bluetooth, ModBus, M-Bus, Wireless M-Bus, Power Line Communication (PLC), 6LoWPAN, IEEE 802.15.4. M2M area networks use proprietary or non-IP protocols locally, whereas the communication network linking remote M2M area networks is IP-based.']],
quiz:[{k:'mcq',q:'MQTT uses which model?',o:['Request–response','Publish–subscribe','Exclusive pair','Push–pull'],a:1},{k:'mcq',q:'Zigbee is based on IEEE…',o:['802.3','802.11','802.15.4','802.16'],a:2},{k:'gap',q:'CoAP is designed for ___-constrained devices.',a:'resource',alt:['resource constrained']},{k:'tf',q:'WebSocket provides full-duplex communication.',a:true},{q:'Which 5G New Radio (5G NR) service pillar is specifically designed for connecting massive densities of low-power IoT sensors?',o:['eMBB (Enhanced Mobile Broadband)','URLLC (Ultra-Reliable Low-Latency Communications)','mMTC (Massive Machine-Type Communications)','WiMAX 802.16m'],a:2,e:'mMTC (Massive Machine-Type Communications) targets high-density IoT sensor deployments; URLLC targets ultra-low-latency mission-critical control; eMBB targets high-bandwidth broadband.',k:'mcq',w:'mMTC (Massive Machine-Type Communications) targets high-density IoT sensor deployments; URLLC targets ultra-low-latency mission-critical control; eMBB targets high-bandwidth broadband.'},{q:'Which statement accurately describes CoAP (Constrained Application Protocol)?',o:['It is a publish-subscribe protocol that runs over TCP and uses XML streams.','It is a request-response web transfer protocol supporting GET, PUT, POST, and DELETE that runs over connectionless UDP datagrams for constrained M2M environments.','It is a link-layer standard operating at 60 GHz.','It is a stateful full-duplex protocol initiated via an HTTP upgrade handshake.'],a:1,e:'Slide 13 defines CoAP as an application-layer request-response protocol for constrained M2M devices that runs on top of UDP and supports GET, PUT, POST, and DELETE.',k:'mcq',w:'Slide 13 defines CoAP as an application-layer request-response protocol for constrained M2M devices that runs on top of UDP and supports GET, PUT, POST, and DELETE.'},{q:'In the logical design of IoT (Lecture 3 Slides 16–17), which functional block provides device discovery, real-time monitoring, control pipelines, and data publishing mechanisms?',o:['Device','Services','Management','Application'],a:1,e:'Slide 16 states that the Services functional block provides foundational utilities including device discovery, real-time monitoring, control pipelines, and data publishing mechanisms.',k:'mcq',w:'Slide 16 states that the Services functional block provides foundational utilities including device discovery, real-time monitoring, control pipelines, and data publishing mechanisms.'},{q:'Which IoT communication model uses queues to decouple data producers from consumers and acts as a buffer when the producer push rate and consumer pull rate do not match?',o:['Request–Response','Publish–Subscribe','Push–Pull','Exclusive Pair'],a:2,e:'Slide 19 explains that in the Push–Pull model, producers push data to queues and consumers pull from queues, buffering rate mismatches.',k:'mcq',w:'Slide 19 explains that in the Push–Pull model, producers push data to queues and consumers pull from queues, buffering rate mismatches.'},{q:'Which of the following is NOT one of the five REST architectural constraints on Lecture 3 Slides 20–22?',o:['Stateless (session state is kept entirely on the client)','Persistent server-side session state (server remembers every open client connection)','Cache-able','Layered System and Uniform Interface'],a:1,e:'REST is strictly stateless (session state is kept on the client); persistent server-tracked connections belong to the Exclusive Pair (WebSocket) model.',k:'mcq',w:'REST is strictly stateless (session state is kept on the client); persistent server-tracked connections belong to the Exclusive Pair (WebSocket) model.'}],
theory:[{q:'Compare request–response and publish–subscribe models and recommend one for 500 temperature sensors reporting to many dashboards.',pts:['Request–response explained','Pub–sub explained','Broker/topic','Decoupling','Recommend MQTT pub–sub','Justify'],model:`<p>Request–response: client asks, server answers; tight coupling. Publish–subscribe: publishers send to broker topics; subscribers receive without knowing each other. For 500 sensors and many dashboards, MQTT pub–sub decouples producers and consumers and saves bandwidth.</p>`},{q:'A smart factory deploys 500 battery-powered vibration and temperature sensors that report small telemetry packets every 30 seconds to three backend services (a live operator dashboard, an alarm engine, and a historical database), while a maintenance engineer also needs a real-time bidirectional control panel to steer a robotic arm. Recommend and justify: (a) the Link and Network layer protocols for the battery sensors; (b) the Application protocol and communication model for the 500 sensors; and (c) the Application API/protocol and communication model for the robotic arm control panel.',model:`<p><b>Background:</b> Designing an industrial IoT communication stack requires matching each layer (Link, Network, Transport, Application) and logical communication model (Request–Response, Publish–Subscribe, Push–Pull, Exclusive Pair) to the power, bandwidth, fan-out, and latency constraints of each subsystem.</p><p><b>Answer and solution:</b></p><ol><li><b>(a) Link &amp; Network Layer for the 500 Battery Sensors — IEEE 802.15.4 + 6LoWPAN:</b><ul><li><b>Link Layer:</b> <b>IEEE 802.15.4 (LR-WPAN)</b> operating at 40–250 Kb/s is specifically engineered for low-cost, low-speed, power-constrained battery devices (unlike power-hungry Wi-Fi 802.11).</li><li><b>Network Layer:</b> <b>6LoWPAN</b> (IPv6 over Low-power Wireless Personal Area Networks) operates at 2.4 GHz (250 Kb/s) over 802.15.4 and applies header compression so full 128-bit IPv6 datagrams fit inside tiny low-power sensor frames.</li></ul></li><li><b>(b) Application Protocol &amp; Model for Telemetry Fan-Out — MQTT (Publish–Subscribe):</b><ul><li><b>Model:</b> <b>Publish–Subscribe</b>. The 500 sensors act as publishers sending telemetry to a topic (e.g., <code>factory/sensors/telemetry</code>) on an <b>MQTT Broker</b>.</li><li><b>Justification:</b> The three backend consumers (dashboard, alarm engine, historical database) all subscribe to the topic and receive every reading simultaneously. Publishers and consumers are completely decoupled, and MQTT's tiny header overhead saves battery and bandwidth compared to polling with HTTP Request–Response. (If any consumer processes data more slowly than sensors publish, a <b>Push–Pull</b> queue can buffer the rate mismatch).</li></ul></li><li><b>(c) Application API &amp; Model for the Live Robotic Arm Panel — WebSocket API (Exclusive Pair):</b><ul><li><b>Model:</b> <b>Exclusive Pair</b> via a <b>WebSocket API</b> over TCP.</li><li><b>Justification:</b> WebSocket begins with an HTTP upgrade handshake and then keeps a single persistent, stateful, <b>full-duplex</b> TCP connection open. Both the engineer's control panel and the robotic arm controller can stream commands and position feedback simultaneously with near-zero latency, avoiding the connection setup/teardown overhead of stateless REST calls.</li></ul></li></ol><p><b>Scenario:</b> When sensor #142 detects bearing overheating, it publishes one compact MQTT packet over 6LoWPAN/802.15.4 to the local gateway broker; within milliseconds the alarm service triggers a warning, the database logs the spike, and the engineer uses the open WebSocket control channel to throttle down the robotic arm in real time.</p><p><b>How they combine:</b> The local gateway bridges the constrained 802.15.4/6LoWPAN sensor mesh to the factory IP network, hosting the MQTT broker for one-to-many telemetry distribution while maintaining dedicated WebSocket streams for low-latency actuator control.</p><p><b>Conclusion:</b> Pairing 802.15.4 + 6LoWPAN at the lower layers with MQTT Publish–Subscribe for many-to-many sensor telemetry and WebSocket Exclusive Pair for bidirectional real-time control satisfies both battery longevity and sub-second industrial responsiveness.</p>`,rubric:['Selects IEEE 802.15.4 (Link) and 6LoWPAN (Network) with data rate (40-250 Kb/s) and IPv6 header compression justification','Recommends MQTT with the Publish-Subscribe model for the 500 sensors reporting to 3 services, explaining broker topic decoupling','Recommends WebSocket API with the Exclusive Pair model for the bidirectional control screen, noting the HTTP upgrade handshake and persistent full-duplex connection'],pts:['Selects IEEE 802.15.4 (Link) and 6LoWPAN (Network) with data rate (40-250 Kb/s) and IPv6 header compression justification','Recommends MQTT with the Publish-Subscribe model for the 500 sensors reporting to 3 services, explaining broker topic decoupling','Recommends WebSocket API with the Exclusive Pair model for the bidirectional control screen, noting the HTTP upgrade handshake and persistent full-duplex connection']}]},
{id:'t4',title:'Embedded systems fundamentals',why:'Microcontroller vs microprocessor, sensors/actuators, ADC.',
cards:[
{h:'Embedded systems',b:`<div class="def"><b>Embedded system</b>A computer system with a dedicated function within a larger device, often with real-time constraints.</div><table><tr><th>Microcontroller (MCU)</th><th>Microprocessor (MPU)</th></tr><tr><td>CPU + RAM + flash + I/O on one chip; low power, cheap (Arduino/ATmega, ESP32)</td><td>CPU only, external memory; runs an OS (Raspberry Pi)</td></tr></table><p><b>Sensors</b> convert physical quantities to signals; <b>actuators</b> turn signals into action (motors, relays). <b>ADC</b> converts analog to digital; GPIO, PWM, I2C, SPI, UART interfaces. <b>RTOS</b> = real-time OS.</p>`},
{h:'General-Purpose vs Embedded Systems & The Input–Processing–Output Model',b:`<table><tr><th>Dimension</th><th>General-Purpose Computer</th><th>Embedded System</th></tr><tr><td><b>Purpose</b></td><td>Designed to perform many different tasks (desktops, laptops)</td><td>Hardware + software integrated into a larger device to perform a <b>specific task or set of closely related functions</b></td></tr><tr><td><b>Software</b></td><td>User can install many varied applications on a GPOS</td><td>Firmware normally designed specifically for the device (bare-metal or RTOS)</td></tr><tr><td><b>Resources</b></td><td>Considerable processing power and memory</td><td>Selected tightly to fit the application's cost, power, and size limits</td></tr></table><p><b>Three Major Functional Stages (Slides 5–8):</b> <code>INPUT &gt;&gt; PROCESSING &gt;&gt; OUTPUT</code></p><ul><li><b>1. INPUT:</b> <i>Physical</i> (temp, pressure, motion, light, sound), <i>Electrical</i> (sensor voltage/current), <i>Digital</i> (data from another device), <i>User-generated</i> (buttons, keypad, touchscreen), or <i>Communication data</i> (UART, SPI, I<sup>2</sup>C, Ethernet, Wi-Fi, Bluetooth).</li><li><b>2. PROCESSING ("Decision-making"):</b> Performed by an <b>MCU, MPU, DSP, FPGA</b>, or dedicated hardware.</li><li><b>3. OUTPUT:</b> Control a physical device, display info, produce an audible signal, send data to another system, store info, or activate an actuator.</li></ul>`},
{h:'The 4 Core Categories: MPU, MCU, DSP, ASIC & PLD/FPGA',b:`<p><b>Four Broad Categories of Embedded Cores (Slide 10):</b> (1) General-purpose &amp; domain-specific processors (MPU, MCU, DSP); (2) Application Specific Integrated Circuits (<b>ASIC</b>); (3) Programmable Logic Devices (<b>PLD</b> — CPLD, FPGA); (4) Commercial Off-The-Shelf components (<b>COTS</b> — written as <i>"CoS"</i> on Slide 10).</p><table><tr><th>Core Type</th><th>Slide Definition, Strengths &amp; Examples</th></tr><tr><td><b>Microprocessor (MPU)</b></td><td>Processor IC that performs computation but <b>requires external memory and I/O interfaces</b> to form a complete system. Used in calculators, accounting systems, game machines, complex industrial controllers, military &amp; comm systems. (e.g., <b>Raspberry Pi 5</b> BCM2712).</td></tr><tr><td><b>Microcontroller (MCU)</b></td><td>Single-chip IC combining <b>processor core + memory (RAM/Flash) + peripheral I/O interfaces</b> for controlling a specific function. Families: <b>ARM Cortex-M, STM32, ESP32, AVR, PIC, 8051</b>. Used in washing machines, cameras, alarms, microwaves.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Digital Signal Processor (DSP)</b></td><td>Processor optimized for fast mathematical operations on digital signals (audio, video, telecommunications). Contains <b>program memory, data memory, computational engine, and I/O unit</b>.</td></tr><tr><td><b>ASIC</b></td><td>Integrated circuit custom-fabricated for one particular application. <b>Pros:</b> highest performance &amp; optimization. <b>Cons:</b> high development cost and cannot be altered after fabrication.</td></tr><tr><td><b>PLD / FPGA</b></td><td>IC whose logic functionality can be programmed/reconfigured by the designer (<b>CPLD, FPGA</b>). An <b>FPGA</b> contains configurable logic resources and interconnections for <b>hardware acceleration, parallel processing, high-speed specialized digital logic, and reconfigurable hardware</b>.</td></tr></table>`},
{h:'Communication Interfaces (Chip-Level vs External) & 3 Classifications',b:`<p><b>Communication Interfaces (Slide 16):</b></p><ul><li><b>Chip-Level Interfaces:</b><ul><li><b>I<sup>2</sup>C (Inter-Integrated Circuit):</b> Two-wire synchronous serial interface designed for communication between ICs on a board.</li><li><b>SPI (Serial Peripheral Interface):</b> Synchronous serial interface with a shared clock for relatively fast communication between a controller and peripherals (displays, sensors, flash memory, ADC/DAC).</li><li><b>UART (Universal Asynchronous Receiver Transmitter):</b> Serial communication <b>without requiring a shared clock signal</b>.</li><li><b>Others on slide:</b> <b>1-Wire</b> and <b>Parallel bus</b>.</li></ul></li><li><b>External Interfaces:</b> <i>Wired:</i> <b>RS-232C, RS-485, parallel port, USB, IEEE 1394 (FireWire)</b>. <i>Wireless:</i> <b>Infrared (IrDA), Bluetooth, Wi-Fi, ZigBee, GPRS</b>.</li></ul><p><b>Three Embedded System Classifications (Slides 17–20):</b></p><ol><li><b>By Triggering:</b> <b>Event-Triggered</b> (dynamic task execution driven by external events) vs <b>Time-Triggered</b> (follows a statically computed schedule with allocated time slots; predictable).</li><li><b>By Deterministic Behaviour (Real-Time):</b> <b>Soft Real-Time</b> (missing a deadline or small errors are not critical and can be tolerated to a degree, e.g., video streaming) vs <b>Hard Real-Time</b> (missing a task execution deadline causes <b>catastrophic consequences</b> such as financial loss or human loss of life, e.g., airbag, pacemaker, aircraft control).</li><li><b>By Complexity &amp; Performance:</b><ul><li><b>Small Scale:</b> 8-bit or 16-bit processor/controller, low cost, not time-critical, may or may not have an OS.</li><li><b>Medium Scale:</b> 16-bit or 32-bit MCU/MPU or DSP, slightly complex hardware/firmware, may run GPOS or RTOS.</li><li><b>Large Scale / Complex:</b> High-performance 32-bit or 64-bit RISC processors, RSoC (Reconfigurable SoC), multi-core processors, PLDs, and hardware accelerators offloading work from the main processor.</li></ul></li></ol>`}],
recall:[['Define an embedded system.','A computer system with a dedicated function inside a larger device.'],['MCU vs MPU?','An MCU integrates CPU, memory and I/O on one chip; an MPU is a CPU needing external memory and usually an OS.'],['Sensor vs actuator?','Sensors measure; actuators act.'],['What does an ADC do?','Converts analog signals into digital values.'],['Name the four broad categories of embedded system cores listed on Lecture 4 Slide 10.','1) General-purpose and domain-specific processors (MPU, MCU, DSP), 2) Application Specific Integrated Circuits (ASIC), 3) Programmable Logic Devices (PLD — CPLD, FPGA), 4) Commercial Off-The-Shelf components (COTS / "CoS").'],['List the six microcontroller families named on Lecture 4 Slide 12 and answer Slide 11\'s question: "Where does Raspberry Pi 5 fall into?"','MCU families: ARM Cortex-M, STM32, ESP32, AVR, PIC, and 8051. The Raspberry Pi 5 is a Single-Board Computer (SBC) built around a microprocessor (Broadcom BCM2712 quad-core Cortex-A76) with external RAM running Linux, rather than a single-chip microcontroller.'],['Compare a DSP, an ASIC, and an FPGA according to Lecture 4 Slides 13–15.','A DSP is a processor with program memory, data memory, computational engine, and I/O optimized for math on digital audio/video/telecom signals. An ASIC is a custom-fabricated chip for one application (high performance, but expensive and fixed after fabrication). An FPGA is a Programmable Logic Device with configurable logic blocks and interconnections for parallel processing, hardware acceleration, and reconfigurable digital hardware.'],['Contrast I2C, SPI, and UART chip-level interfaces.','I2C is a serial interface for chip-to-chip communication; SPI is a synchronous serial interface for fast communication with displays, sensors, flash memory, and ADC/DACs; UART is an asynchronous serial interface that operates without a shared clock signal.'],['How are embedded systems classified by (a) triggering, (b) deterministic behaviour, and (c) complexity/performance?','(a) Triggering: Event-triggered (dynamic, event-driven) vs Time-triggered (statically computed schedule/time slots, predictable). (b) Determinism: Soft real-time (missed deadlines tolerable) vs Hard real-time (missed deadlines cause catastrophic financial or human life loss). (c) Complexity: Small scale (8/16-bit), Medium scale (16/32-bit or DSP, GPOS/RTOS), Large scale (32/64-bit RISC, RSoC, multi-core, PLD/accelerators).']],
quiz:[{k:'mcq',q:'Which is a microcontroller board?',o:['Raspberry Pi 5','Arduino Uno','Laptop','GPU'],a:1},{k:'gap',q:'Dimming an LED by switching rapidly uses ___.',a:'pwm'},{k:'tf',q:'A relay is an actuator.',a:true},{q:'Which chip-level communication interface provides serial communication WITHOUT requiring a shared clock signal?',o:['SPI (Serial Peripheral Interface)','I2C (Inter-Integrated Circuit)','UART (Universal Asynchronous Receiver Transmitter)','Parallel bus'],a:2,e:'Slide 16 states that UART (Universal Asynchronous Receiver Transmitter) provides serial communication without requiring a shared clock signal.',k:'mcq',w:'Slide 16 states that UART (Universal Asynchronous Receiver Transmitter) provides serial communication without requiring a shared clock signal.'},{q:'An automotive airbag controller and an implantable cardiac pacemaker must execute their safety tasks within strict millisecond deadlines or human lives could be lost. How are they classified?',o:['Soft real-time, small-scale systems','Hard real-time systems','Non-deterministic general-purpose systems','Best-effort cloud batch systems'],a:1,e:'Slide 18 defines Hard Real-Time systems as those where missing a task execution deadline can have catastrophic consequences (financial loss, human loss of life).',k:'mcq',w:'Slide 18 defines Hard Real-Time systems as those where missing a task execution deadline can have catastrophic consequences (financial loss, human loss of life).'},{q:'Which embedded core category offers custom hardware acceleration, parallel processing, and digital logic that can still be reconfigured by the designer after manufacturing?',o:['ASIC (Application Specific Integrated Circuit)','FPGA (Field-Programmable Gate Array, a type of PLD)','8051 8-bit Microcontroller','RS-232C Serial Port'],a:1,e:'Slide 15 explains that an FPGA (a Programmable Logic Device) has configurable logic resources and interconnections for hardware acceleration, parallel processing, and reconfigurable hardware, whereas an ASIC is fixed after fabrication.',k:'mcq',w:'Slide 15 explains that an FPGA (a Programmable Logic Device) has configurable logic resources and interconnections for hardware acceleration, parallel processing, and reconfigurable hardware, whereas an ASIC is fixed after fabrication.'},{q:'According to Lecture 4 Slide 20, which processor architectures are used in Large-Scale / Complex embedded systems?',o:['8-bit PIC and 8051 microcontrollers without an OS','High-performance 32- or 64-bit RISC processors/controllers, RSoC, multi-core processors, PLDs, and hardware accelerators','Only passive analog resistors and capacitors','16-bit calculators only'],a:1,e:'Slide 20 defines Large-Scale/Complex embedded systems around 32- or 64-bit RISC processors, RSoC, multi-core processors, PLDs, and co-units/hardware accelerators.',k:'mcq',w:'Slide 20 defines Large-Scale/Complex embedded systems around 32- or 64-bit RISC processors, RSoC, multi-core processors, PLDs, and co-units/hardware accelerators.'}],
theory:[{q:'Distinguish microcontrollers from microprocessors and explain the role of sensors, actuators and ADCs in an embedded system.',pts:['MCU','MPU','Examples','Sensors','Actuators','ADC','Interfaces'],model:`<p>An MCU (ESP32) integrates CPU, memory and I/O for dedicated low-power tasks; an MPU (Raspberry Pi) needs external memory and runs an OS. Sensors measure, the ADC digitises analog readings, the MCU decides, and actuators act, connected through GPIO, I2C, SPI or UART.</p>`},{q:'Compare the five embedded processing core options covered in Lecture 4 (Microprocessor, Microcontroller, DSP, ASIC, and PLD/FPGA), and explain how embedded systems are classified by triggering mechanism, real-time determinism, and complexity/performance scale.',model:`<p><b>Background:</b> Every embedded system follows the <code>INPUT &gt;&gt; PROCESSING &gt;&gt; OUTPUT</code> model inside a host product. Selecting the right computational core and real-time architecture determines whether the device meets its latency deadlines, power envelope, and manufacturing budget.</p><p><b>Answer and solution:</b></p><p><b>1. Comparison of Embedded Processing Cores (Slides 10–15):</b></p><ul><li><b>Microprocessor (MPU):</b> A CPU integrated circuit that requires external RAM, storage, and I/O chips (e.g., Raspberry Pi 5's Cortex-A76 running Linux); best for complex OS-driven controllers and data acquisition.</li><li><b>Microcontroller (MCU):</b> Combines the CPU core, RAM, Flash/ROM, and I/O peripherals on a single chip (e.g., ARM Cortex-M, STM32, ESP32, AVR, PIC, 8051); best for low-power dedicated control (appliances, sensors).</li><li><b>Digital Signal Processor (DSP):</b> Contains separate program memory, data memory, computational engine, and I/O optimized for high-speed math on audio, video, and telecommunications signals.</li><li><b>Application Specific Integrated Circuit (ASIC):</b> Custom-fabricated chip tailored to one function; delivers maximum speed and energy efficiency but has high non-recurring development cost and zero post-fabrication flexibility.</li><li><b>Programmable Logic Device (PLD / CPLD / FPGA):</b> Contains configurable logic blocks and interconnections that can be reprogrammed in the field for parallel hardware acceleration and high-speed custom digital logic.</li></ul><p><b>2. Three Classifications of Embedded Systems (Slides 17–20):</b></p><ol><li><b>By Triggering:</b> <i>Event-triggered</i> systems execute dynamically in response to external interrupts/events (e.g., button press or motion alarm), whereas <i>Time-triggered</i> systems execute tasks in statically computed time slots for strict predictability.</li><li><b>By Deterministic Behaviour:</b> <i>Hard real-time</i> systems cannot miss a deadline without catastrophic financial or life-safety consequences (e.g., car airbag), whereas <i>Soft real-time</i> systems tolerate occasional deadline misses with graceful quality degradation (e.g., video playback).</li><li><b>By Complexity &amp; Performance:</b> <i>Small-scale</i> (8/16-bit MCUs, simple tasks, often bare-metal), <i>Medium-scale</i> (16/32-bit MCUs or DSPs running an RTOS/GPOS), and <i>Large-scale</i> (32/64-bit multi-core RISC, RSoC, PLDs, and hardware accelerators).</li></ol><p><b>Scenario:</b> In an autonomous drone, a <b>small/medium-scale STM32 MCU</b> runs a <b>time-triggered, hard real-time</b> flight-stabilization loop reading gyroscopes over <b>SPI</b>, while an onboard <b>FPGA/DSP</b> accelerates camera obstacle-avoidance vision in parallel.</p><p><b>How they combine:</b> High-speed synchronous <b>SPI</b> and two-wire <b>I<sup>2</sup>C</b> buses link the sensors and ADC/DACs to the MCU/FPGA cores, allowing the hard real-time control loop and the parallel signal-processing pipeline to coordinate seamlessly.</p><p><b>Conclusion:</b> Matching the processing core (MCU, MPU, DSP, ASIC, FPGA) with the appropriate triggering and real-time classification prevents both hardware over-engineering and catastrophic deadline failures.</p>`,rubric:['Distinguishes MPU, MCU (naming families like STM32/ESP32/AVR/PIC/8051), DSP (four internal blocks), ASIC (pros/cons), and PLD/FPGA (parallel reconfigurable logic)','Explains Event-triggered vs Time-triggered and Soft vs Hard real-time systems with examples','Contrasts Small-scale (8/16-bit), Medium-scale (16/32-bit + DSP + RTOS), and Large-scale (32/64-bit RISC + RSoC + PLD accelerators)'],pts:['Distinguishes MPU, MCU (naming families like STM32/ESP32/AVR/PIC/8051), DSP (four internal blocks), ASIC (pros/cons), and PLD/FPGA (parallel reconfigurable logic)','Explains Event-triggered vs Time-triggered and Soft vs Hard real-time systems with examples','Contrasts Small-scale (8/16-bit), Medium-scale (16/32-bit + DSP + RTOS), and Large-scale (32/64-bit RISC + RSoC + PLD accelerators)']}]},
{id:'t5',title:'Cloud provisioning and deployment on Azure',why:'IaaS/PaaS/SaaS, Azure IoT Hub and deployment steps.',
cards:[
{h:'Service models',b:`<table><tr><th>Model</th><th>You manage</th><th>Example</th></tr><tr><td>IaaS</td><td>OS, runtime, app</td><td>Azure VM</td></tr><tr><td>PaaS</td><td>App and data</td><td>Azure App Service</td></tr><tr><td>SaaS</td><td>Nothing (just use)</td><td>Microsoft 365</td></tr></table><p>Deployment models: public, private, hybrid, community.</p>`},
{h:'Azure for IoT',b:`<p><b>Azure IoT Hub</b>: secure bidirectional device communication and device registry. <b>IoT Edge</b>: runs cloud workloads (containers) on edge devices. Stream Analytics, Functions, Cosmos DB, Blob Storage. Provisioning: resource group → IoT Hub → register device → connection string → send telemetry. Infrastructure as Code: ARM/Bicep, Terraform.</p>`},
{h:'Azure Provisioning Terminologies & 5 Infrastructure Components',b:`<p><b>Nine Key Terminologies (Lecture 8 Slide 2):</b></p><ul><li><b>Infrastructure Provisioning:</b> Creating and configuring VMs, networks, storage, and access policies.</li><li><b>Azure Subscription:</b> Provides authenticated and authorized access to an Azure account (billing &amp; quota boundary).</li><li><b>Resource:</b> An individually managed Azure service instance (e.g., a VM or storage account).</li><li><b>Resource Group:</b> A logical container to manage and aggregate related resources as a single unit.</li><li><b>Region:</b> A geographic area containing Azure data centres (e.g., <i>South Africa North</i>).</li><li><b>Availability Zone:</b> A physically separate data-centre location within a supported region designed to improve resilience against power/cooling/network failures.</li><li><b>Infrastructure as Code (IaC):</b> Defining infrastructure in version-controlled, repeatable files instead of manual clicks.</li><li><b>Telemetry:</b> Measurements or events produced by devices and systems (temperature, device status).</li><li><b>ARM (Azure Resource Manager):</b> The management layer that enables creating, updating, and deleting resources in an Azure subscription.</li></ul><p><b>Five Infrastructure Components &amp; Exact Azure Examples (Slide 3):</b></p><table><tr><th>Component</th><th>Purpose</th><th>Azure Examples on Slide</th></tr><tr><td><b>Compute</b></td><td>Runs OS, app code, and processing jobs</td><td><b>Azure Virtual Machines, Container Apps, AKS, Azure Functions</b></td></tr><tr><td><b>Storage</b></td><td>Persists files, objects, databases, and telemetry</td><td><b>Azure Blob Storage, Azure Files, Azure SQL Database</b></td></tr><tr><td><b>Networking</b></td><td>Connects resources and controls traffic</td><td><b>Virtual Network (VNet), Subnets, Network Security Groups (NSGs), Load Balancer</b></td></tr><tr><td><b>Identity &amp; Security</b></td><td>Controls who or what can access resources</td><td><b>Microsoft Entra ID, Azure RBAC, Managed Identities, Key Vault</b></td></tr><tr><td><b>Monitoring</b></td><td>Collects logs, metrics, traces, and alerts</td><td><b>Azure Monitor, Log Analytics, Application Insights</b></td></tr></table>`},
{h:'Shared Responsibility, 7 Provisioning Steps & Manual vs Automated Approaches',b:`<p><b>Shared Responsibility Model (Slide 4):</b> Azure operates the physical cloud infrastructure, while customers remain responsible for configuration decisions, identities, data, app code, and permissions:</p><ul><li><b>IaaS:</b> Customer manages <b>OS configuration and patching</b>, application software, identities, data, and network rules.</li><li><b>PaaS:</b> Customer manages application code, data, identities, and service configuration; Azure manages the OS and platform.</li><li><b>Managed Containers / Serverless:</b> Customer manages application logic, container images or function code, permissions, data, and deployment settings.</li></ul><p><b>Seven Provisioning Steps on Azure (Slide 5):</b> <b>1. Gather requirements</b> (workload, capacity, region, security, availability, budget) &rarr; <b>2. Design architecture</b> &rarr; <b>3. Organize resources</b> (subscriptions, resource groups, naming conventions, tags) &rarr; <b>4. Define configuration</b> &rarr; <b>5. Provision resources</b> (Portal, CLI, PowerShell, ARM/Bicep) &rarr; <b>6. Validate</b> (connectivity, permissions, health, security) &rarr; <b>7. Operate</b> (monitor, patch, back up, scale, review costs, remove unused resources).</p><table><tr><th>Approach (Slide 6)</th><th>Strengths</th><th>Limitations</th></tr><tr><td><b>Azure Portal</b></td><td>Accessible for learning and one-off tasks</td><td>Harder to reproduce exactly; risk of human error and <b>configuration drift</b></td></tr><tr><td><b>Azure CLI / PowerShell</b></td><td>Scriptable and useful for repeatable operations</td><td>Scripts require testing, versioning, and secure parameter handling</td></tr><tr><td><b>ARM Templates / Bicep</b></td><td><b>Declarative, reviewable, and repeatable</b> infrastructure</td><td>Requires template design, validation, and lifecycle management</td></tr><tr><td><b>CI/CD Pipeline</b></td><td>Automates checks and controlled deployments</td><td>Pipeline permissions and secrets must be secured</td></tr></table>`},
{h:'VM Design Decisions, Security Checklist, Scalability vs Elasticity & VMs vs Containers',b:`<ul><li><b>Seven VM Design Decisions (Slide 7):</b> <b>1. OS</b> (Windows/Linux image) &middot; <b>2. Size</b> (CPU, RAM, storage throughput, network capacity) &middot; <b>3. Disks</b> (distinguish <b>OS disk, temporary disk, and persistent data disks</b>; plan backup/recovery) &middot; <b>4. Networking</b> (VNet, subnets, restrict inbound access) &middot; <b>5. Identity</b> (prefer <b>managed identities</b>; avoid embedding credentials in scripts) &middot; <b>6. Availability</b> (availability zones, availability sets, or VM scale sets) &middot; <b>7. Operations</b> (patching, monitoring, backups, cost controls).</li><li><b>Six-Item VM Security Checklist (Slide 9):</b> (1) Avoid exposing <b>SSH (TCP 22) or RDP (TCP 3389)</b> broadly to the internet; (2) Use controlled admin access, strong auth, and <b>least privilege</b>; (3) Apply OS and app security updates; (4) Use <b>Network Security Groups (NSGs)</b> and network segmentation; (5) Enable logging, monitoring, backup, and alerting; (6) Store secrets in a dedicated secret-management service (<b>Azure Key Vault</b>) rather than source code.</li><li><b>Scalability vs Elasticity (Slide 8):</b> <b>Scalability</b> is the ability to handle increased workload by adding resources or capacity. <b>Elasticity</b> is the ability to adjust resources dynamically as demand changes — <b>including scaling down automatically when demand falls</b>. <b style="color:#b91c1c">Exam Rule:</b> <i>"A system can scale without being elastic if capacity is increased manually and never reduced automatically."</i></li></ul><table><tr><th>Characteristic (Slide 10)</th><th>Virtual Machine (VM)</th><th>Container</th></tr><tr><td><b>Operating System</b></td><td>Each VM has its own full <b>guest OS</b> on virtualized hardware</td><td>Containers <b>share the host OS kernel</b> and isolate application processes</td></tr><tr><td><b>Startup &amp; Footprint</b></td><td>Heavier and slower to start</td><td>Lighter and faster to start</td></tr><tr><td><b>Isolation &amp; Best Fit</b></td><td>Virtualized hardware boundary; best for OS-level control, legacy workloads, custom kernels</td><td>Process-level isolation; best for portable app packaging and repeatable releases</td></tr><tr><td><b>Azure Examples</b></td><td><b>Azure Virtual Machines, VM Scale Sets</b></td><td><b>Azure Container Apps, AKS, Azure Container Instances</b></td></tr></table>`},
{h:'CA2 Practical Walkthrough: AlmaLinux VM & Apache on Azure',b:`<p>In the CA2 Practical Task, you deployed <b><code>PAUCSC418-AlmaLinux-VM</code></b> in resource group <b><code>PAUCSC418-RG</code></b> (<b>South Africa North, Availability Zone 1</b>, size <b><code>Standard_B2ats_v2</code></b>: 2 vCPUs, 1 GiB RAM; 30 GiB Standard SSD OS disk):</p><ul><li><b>NSG Ports:</b> <b>TCP 22 (SSH)</b> for remote CLI administration (restrict to trusted IPs to prevent automated brute-force scans); <b>TCP 80 (HTTP)</b> for plaintext web traffic; <b>TCP 443 (HTTPS)</b> for TLS-encrypted web traffic.</li><li><b>Why opening Port 443 in NSG is NOT enough for HTTPS:</b> The NSG only lets packets reach the VM; Apache also needs an <b>SSL/TLS certificate</b> (e.g., Let's Encrypt), <b><code>mod_ssl</code></b> installed/configured, and Apache listening on port 443.</li><li><b>Linux Verification Commands:</b> <code>whoami</code> (current user), <code>hostname</code>, <code>cat /etc/os-release</code> (AlmaLinux 9.9), <code>free -h</code> (~1 GiB RAM), <code>df -h</code> (30 GiB root disk), <code>ip addr</code> (shows only private IP <code>172.16.0.4</code> on <code>eth0</code> because Azure maps the public IP <code>102.37.17.124</code> at the network edge via <b>NAT</b>!), <code>uptime</code>.</li><li><b>Apache Setup &amp; SELinux:</b><pre>sudo dnf update -y &amp;&amp; sudo dnf install httpd php -y
sudo systemctl enable --now httpd   # enable = start at boot; start/--now = run immediately
sudo cp ~/index.php /var/www/html/
sudo restorecon -Rv /var/www/html   # restore SELinux security contexts
sudo systemctl restart httpd</pre></li><li><b>Public IP vs Azure DNS Name:</b> A dynamic public IP is hard to remember and can change when a VM is deallocated; an Azure DNS label (e.g., <code>myvm.southafricanorth.cloudapp.azure.com</code>) gives a stable, human-readable FQDN.</li></ul>`}],
recall:[['IaaS vs PaaS vs SaaS?','IaaS provides infrastructure; PaaS a platform for your app; SaaS a finished application.'],['What does Azure IoT Hub do?','Manages devices and secure two-way communication between devices and the cloud.'],['What is Azure IoT Edge?','A service that deploys cloud workloads as containers to edge devices.'],['Steps to connect a device to IoT Hub.','Create resource group, create IoT Hub, register device, get connection string, send telemetry.'],['Distinguish between an Azure Region, an Availability Zone, and a Resource Group.','A Region is a geographic area containing Azure data centres (e.g., South Africa North). An Availability Zone is a physically separate data-centre location within a supported region with independent power/cooling/networking for fault resilience. A Resource Group is a logical container used to manage and aggregate related Azure resources as a single unit.'],['According to Lecture 8 Slide 8, what is the exact difference between Scalability and Elasticity, and can a system be scalable without being elastic?','Scalability is the ability to handle increased workload by adding resources or capacity. Elasticity is the ability to adjust resources automatically as demand changes, including scaling down when demand falls. Yes: a system scales without being elastic if capacity is increased manually and never reduced automatically.'],['Under the Lecture 8 Shared Responsibility Model, how does customer responsibility differ across IaaS, PaaS, and Managed Containers/Serverless?','In IaaS (Azure VMs), the customer is responsible for OS configuration and patching, application software, identities, data, and network rules. In PaaS, Azure manages the OS and platform while the customer manages app code, data, identities, and service config. In Managed Containers/Serverless, the customer manages app logic, container images/function code, permissions, data, and deployment settings.'],['Compare Virtual Machines and Containers across OS architecture, startup speed, isolation boundary, and Azure service examples.','VMs run a full guest OS per VM on virtualized hardware (heavier, slower startup, hardware-boundary isolation; e.g., Azure Virtual Machines, VM Scale Sets). Containers share the host OS kernel and isolate application processes (lighter, faster startup, portable packaging; e.g., Azure Container Apps, AKS, Azure Container Instances).'],['In the CA2 Azure Linux practical, why does running ip addr inside the VM show only the private IP (172.16.0.4) and not the public IP (102.37.17.124), and what is the difference between systemctl start httpd and systemctl enable httpd?','Azure binds the public IP at the virtual network edge and translates it to the NIC\'s private IP via NAT, so the guest OS only sees its private IP on eth0. systemctl start httpd starts the service immediately for the current session only, whereas systemctl enable httpd configures it to start automatically at boot (systemctl enable --now httpd does both).'],['In the CA2 practical, why does allowing inbound TCP port 443 in the Azure Network Security Group (NSG) not automatically make your Apache website work over HTTPS?','An NSG is only a network firewall that allows packets on port 443 to reach the VM. For HTTPS to work, Apache inside the VM must also be configured to listen on port 443 with mod_ssl installed and a valid SSL/TLS certificate.']],
quiz:[{k:'mcq',q:'Hosting a web app without managing servers is…',o:['IaaS','PaaS','SaaS','On-premise'],a:1},{k:'tf',q:'Terraform is an Infrastructure as Code tool.',a:true},{q:'An administrator manually resizes an Azure VM to handle holiday traffic and leaves it at the larger size permanently. According to Lecture 8 Slide 8, this system demonstrates:',o:['Elasticity without scalability','Scalability without elasticity','Both full elasticity and serverless auto-scaling','Neither scalability nor availability'],a:1,e:'Slide 8 explicitly states: "A system can scale without being elastic if capacity is increased manually and never reduced automatically."',k:'mcq',w:'Slide 8 explicitly states: "A system can scale without being elastic if capacity is increased manually and never reduced automatically."'},{q:'Under the Azure Shared Responsibility Model (Lecture 8 Slide 4), in which service model is the CUSTOMER responsible for operating-system configuration and OS security patching?',o:['IaaS (e.g., Azure Virtual Machines)','PaaS (e.g., Azure App Service)','Serverless (e.g., Azure Functions)','SaaS (e.g., Microsoft 365)'],a:0,e:'In IaaS, the customer manages the guest OS (including patching), runtime, applications, identities, network rules, and data.',k:'mcq',w:'In IaaS, the customer manages the guest OS (including patching), runtime, applications, identities, network rules, and data.'},{q:'Which Azure provisioning approach on Lecture 8 Slide 6 is described as "Declarative, reviewable and repeatable infrastructure" that avoids manual configuration drift?',o:['Clicking manually through the Azure Portal','ARM templates / Bicep','Unversioned ad-hoc SSH commands','Azure Monitor alerts'],a:1,e:'Slide 6 lists "Declarative, reviewable and repeatable infrastructure" as the key strength of ARM templates / Bicep (Infrastructure as Code).',k:'mcq',w:'Slide 6 lists "Declarative, reviewable and repeatable infrastructure" as the key strength of ARM templates / Bicep (Infrastructure as Code).'},{q:'How do Containers differ from Virtual Machines on Lecture 8 Slide 10?',o:['Each container boots its own separate guest OS kernel, making containers slower than VMs.','Containers share the host operating-system kernel and isolate application processes, making them lighter and faster to start than full VMs.','Containers cannot run on Microsoft Azure.','Virtual machines use process-level isolation while containers virtualize physical hardware.'],a:1,e:'Slide 10 explains that a VM virtualizes a complete OS environment (each VM has its own guest OS), whereas containers share the host OS kernel and isolate application processes.',k:'mcq',w:'Slide 10 explains that a VM virtualizes a complete OS environment (each VM has its own guest OS), whereas containers share the host OS kernel and isolate application processes.'},{q:'In the CA2 AlmaLinux Azure deployment, which command BOTH starts the Apache web server immediately AND configures it to start automatically on every future reboot?',o:['sudo systemctl status httpd','sudo systemctl start httpd','sudo systemctl enable --now httpd','sudo restorecon -Rv /var/www/html'],a:2,e:'`systemctl enable --now httpd` combines `enable` (start at boot) and `start` (run right now).',k:'mcq',w:'`systemctl enable --now httpd` combines `enable` (start at boot) and `start` (run right now).'}],
theory:[{q:'Describe how to provision Azure resources for an IoT temperature-monitoring system.',pts:['Resource group','IoT Hub','Device registration','Telemetry','Processing (Stream Analytics/Functions)','Storage + dashboard','Security'],model:`<p>Create a resource group and IoT Hub; register each sensor and use its connection string; devices send telemetry; Stream Analytics or Functions process it; results go to Cosmos DB and a dashboard; access is secured with keys/certificates and IaC templates make it repeatable.</p>`},{q:'An IoT logistics startup wants to deploy its cloud backend on Microsoft Azure. (a) Map the five infrastructure component categories from Lecture 8 Slide 3 to concrete Azure services. (b) Compare manual Azure Portal provisioning against ARM Templates/Bicep (IaC). (c) Compare deploying their microservices on Azure Virtual Machines vs Containers, and explain the difference between Scalability and Elasticity when traffic spikes every morning.',model:`<p><b>Background:</b> Provisioning a production IoT cloud backend on Microsoft Azure requires organizing compute, storage, networking, identity, and monitoring resources inside a subscription and resource group while balancing operational overhead under the Shared Responsibility Model.</p><p><b>Answer and solution:</b></p><p><b>(a) Five Azure Infrastructure Components &amp; Services (Slide 3):</b></p><ol><li><b>Compute (runs OS, app code, processing jobs):</b> Azure Virtual Machines, Azure Container Apps, Azure Kubernetes Service (AKS), or Azure Functions.</li><li><b>Storage (persists files, objects, databases, telemetry):</b> Azure Blob Storage, Azure Files, or Azure SQL Database.</li><li><b>Networking (connects resources and controls traffic):</b> Virtual Network (VNet), Subnets, Network Security Groups (NSGs), and Azure Load Balancer.</li><li><b>Identity &amp; Security (controls access and secrets):</b> Microsoft Entra ID, Azure Role-Based Access Control (RBAC), Managed Identities, and Azure Key Vault.</li><li><b>Monitoring (collects logs, metrics, traces, alerts):</b> Azure Monitor, Log Analytics, and Application Insights.</li></ol><p><b>(b) Manual Portal Provisioning vs ARM Templates / Bicep (IaC — Slide 6):</b></p><ul><li><b>Azure Portal:</b> Easy for learning and one-off tasks, but difficult to reproduce across dev/staging/prod, prone to human error, and suffers from <b>configuration drift</b>.</li><li><b>ARM Templates / Bicep (Infrastructure as Code):</b> Defines infrastructure declaratively in version-controlled files that can be peer-reviewed, tested in CI/CD pipelines, and deployed repeatably with zero drift.</li></ul><p><b>(c) Virtual Machines vs Containers &amp; Scalability vs Elasticity (Slides 8 &amp; 10):</b></p><ul><li><b>VMs vs Containers:</b> Each <b>VM</b> runs its own full guest OS on virtualized hardware (heavier footprint, slower startup, customer must patch the guest OS under IaaS; best for legacy software or custom OS kernels). <b>Containers</b> share the host OS kernel and isolate application processes (lightweight, start in seconds, package app + dependencies portably; deployed on <b>Azure Container Apps</b> or <b>AKS</b>).</li><li><b>Scalability vs Elasticity:</b> <b>Scalability</b> is adding capacity to handle growth; <b>Elasticity</b> is automatically scaling out during the 8 a.m. telemetry spike <i>and</i> scaling back down when traffic drops at night. Manually adding VMs and never shrinking them is scalable but <b>not elastic</b>.</li></ul><p><b>Scenario:</b> During the 8 a.m. rush when 20,000 delivery trucks start streaming GPS telemetry, hosting the ingestion API on <b>Azure Container Apps</b> defined via <b>Bicep</b> allows Azure to elastically spin up container replicas in seconds and scale them back to zero/minimum off-peak, while authenticating to <b>Azure SQL Database</b> using passwordless <b>Managed Identities</b> and <b>Key Vault</b>.</p><p><b>How they combine:</b> Bicep templates provision the VNet, NSGs, Storage, and Container Apps inside one Resource Group via Azure Resource Manager (ARM), while Azure Monitor and Application Insights feed live metrics to trigger elastic autoscale rules.</p><p><b>Conclusion:</b> Combining declarative IaC (Bicep) with containerized compute and elastic autoscaling minimizes IaaS patching overhead, prevents configuration drift, and aligns cloud cost strictly with live IoT demand.</p>`,rubric:['Lists all five infrastructure component categories from Slide 3 with accurate Azure service examples','Contrasts Azure Portal (configuration drift, manual error) with ARM Templates/Bicep (declarative, version-controlled, repeatable IaC)','Compares VMs (guest OS per VM, IaaS patching) vs Containers (shared host kernel, fast startup) and distinguishes Scalability from Elasticity'],pts:['Lists all five infrastructure component categories from Slide 3 with accurate Azure service examples','Contrasts Azure Portal (configuration drift, manual error) with ARM Templates/Bicep (declarative, version-controlled, repeatable IaC)','Compares VMs (guest OS per VM, IaaS patching) vs Containers (shared host kernel, fast startup) and distinguishes Scalability from Elasticity']}]},
{id:'t6',title:'Choosing the right board',why:'Scenario question: pick a board and justify.',
cards:[
{h:'Selection criteria',b:`<p>Processing power · memory · power consumption/battery · connectivity (Wi-Fi, BLE, LoRa, cellular) · I/O pins and interfaces · size · cost · OS needs · ecosystem/community · environment (temperature, dust).</p><table><tr><th>Board</th><th>Best for</th></tr><tr><td>Arduino Uno</td><td>Simple sensing, learning, low power</td></tr><tr><td>ESP32</td><td>Wi-Fi/BLE IoT nodes, cheap</td></tr><tr><td>Raspberry Pi</td><td>Linux, cameras, gateways, heavier processing</td></tr><tr><td>NVIDIA Jetson</td><td>Edge AI, computer vision</td></tr><tr><td>STM32</td><td>Industrial real-time control</td></tr></table><div class="trap"><b>Additional context:</b> Lecture 9 covers four specific boards in depth — <b>Arduino Uno (R3 &amp; R4 Minima)</b>, <b>Arduino Mega 2560</b>, <b>Raspberry Pi 5</b>, and <b>ESP32</b> (STM32 is listed in Lecture 4 as an MCU family; NVIDIA Jetson is an external industry example for GPU-accelerated edge AI).</div>`},
{h:'MCU vs SBC & The 5 Hardware Selection Parameters',b:`<p><b>Preventing Hardware Misallocation (Lecture 9 Slide 2):</b> Before picking a board, engineers must decide between a <b>Microcontroller Unit (MCU)</b> (Arduino Uno, Arduino Mega 2560, ESP32) and a <b>Single-Board Computer (SBC)</b> (Raspberry Pi 5). Using a Linux SBC for simple LED/relay control wastes power, cost, and complexity; using an 8-bit MCU for computer vision or local databases fails due to tiny RAM and clock speed.</p><p><b>The Five Board Selection Parameters (Slide 3):</b></p><ol><li><b>Processing Power</b> (CPU bit-width, clock speed, cores, RAM, Flash)</li><li><b>Connectivity</b> (USB-only vs on-chip Wi-Fi, Bluetooth/BLE, Gigabit Ethernet)</li><li><b>GPIO &amp; Hardware Interfaces</b> (Digital I/O count, PWM pins, ADC/DAC channels, UART/SPI/I<sup>2</sup>C buses, 5V vs 3.3V logic level)</li><li><b>Community &amp; Ease of Learning</b> (Beginner bare-metal Arduino IDE vs Linux OS terminal &amp; administration)</li><li><b>Power Consumption</b> (Milliamps &amp; microamp deep sleep for batteries vs multi-watt active cooling on mains power)</li></ol>`},
{h:'Exact Hardware Specs: Arduino Uno (R3 vs R4), Mega 2560, Raspberry Pi 5 & ESP32',b:`<table><tr><th>Parameter</th><th>Arduino Uno (R3 &amp; R4 Minima)</th><th>Arduino Mega 2560</th><th>ESP32 (Espressif SoC)</th><th>Raspberry Pi 5 (SBC)</th></tr><tr><td><b>Category &amp; Ease</b></td><td>8-bit MCU (R3) / 32-bit MCU (R4) &middot; <b>High</b> ease</td><td>8-bit MCU &middot; <b>High</b> ease (same workflow as Uno)</td><td>32-bit Wireless SoC MCU &middot; <b>Moderate</b> ease (3.3V quirks)</td><td>64-bit Linux SBC &middot; <b>Moderate–Advanced</b> ease</td></tr><tr><td><b>CPU &amp; Clock</b></td><td><b>R3:</b> ATmega328P (8-bit AVR RISC) @ <b>16 MHz</b><br><b>R4 Minima:</b> Renesas RA4M1 (32-bit Arm Cortex-M4 w/ FPU) @ <b>48 MHz</b></td><td><b>ATmega2560</b> (8-bit AVR RISC) @ <b>16 MHz</b></td><td><b>Tensilica Xtensa Dual-Core 32-bit LX6</b> @ up to <b>240 MHz</b> + <b>ULP co-processor</b></td><td><b>Broadcom BCM2712 Quad-core 64-bit Arm Cortex-A76</b> @ <b>2.4 GHz</b></td></tr><tr><td><b>Memory &amp; Storage</b></td><td><b>R3:</b> <b>2 KB SRAM, 32 KB Flash</b><br><b>R4:</b> 32 KB SRAM, 256 KB Flash</td><td><b>8 KB SRAM, 256 KB Flash, 4 KB EEPROM</b></td><td><b>520 KB SRAM</b> + <b>4 MB to 16 MB</b> external SPI Flash</td><td><b>4 GB, 8 GB, or 16 GB LPDDR4X SDRAM</b>; MicroSD / <b>PCIe 2.0 NVMe SSD</b></td></tr><tr><td><b>Connectivity</b></td><td>USB Type-B/C only. <b>No native Wi-Fi/BT</b> (needs shields)</td><td>USB Type-B only. <b>No native Wi-Fi/BT</b> (needs shields)</td><td>On-chip <b>Wi-Fi 802.11 b/g/n (150 Mbps)</b> + <b>Bluetooth v4.2 BR/EDR &amp; BLE</b></td><td><b>Dual-band 802.11ac Wi-Fi</b>, <b>BT 5.0 &amp; BLE</b>, <b>Gigabit Ethernet (PoE+ via HAT)</b>, 2&times;USB 3.0, 2&times;USB 2.0, 2&times;4K60 micro-HDMI, PCIe 2.0</td></tr><tr><td><b>GPIO, ADC &amp; Logic Level</b></td><td><b>14 Digital I/O (6 PWM)</b>, <b>6 Analog inputs (ADC)</b> &middot; <b>5V logic</b></td><td><b>54 Digital I/O (15 PWM)</b>, <b>16 Analog inputs (10-bit ADC)</b> &middot; <b>5V logic</b></td><td><b>~34 Digital GPIO</b>, <b>18-ch 12-bit ADC</b>, <b>2-ch 8-bit DAC</b>, capacitive touch &middot; <b style="color:#b91c1c">3.3V logic (NOT 5V tolerant!)</b></td><td><b>40-pin GPIO header</b>, 2&times;4-lane MIPI camera/display &middot; <b style="color:#b91c1c">NO onboard ADC!</b> &middot; <b>3.3V logic</b></td></tr><tr><td><b>Bus Interfaces</b></td><td><b>1&times; UART, 1&times; SPI, 1&times; I<sup>2</sup>C</b></td><td><b>4&times; Hardware UART, 1&times; SPI, 1&times; I<sup>2</sup>C</b></td><td><b>3&times; UART, 2&times; SPI, 2&times; I<sup>2</sup>C, 2&times; I<sup>2</sup>S</b> (digital audio)</td><td>Software/hardware configurable UART, SPI, I<sup>2</sup>C, I<sup>2</sup>S</td></tr><tr><td><b>Power Draw</b></td><td><b>~45 mA (~0.22 W)</b> at 5V DC; sleep down to &mu;A</td><td><b>~70 mA (~0.35 W)</b> at 5V DC (higher due to USB chip &amp; larger breakout)</td><td>Active Wi-Fi TX: <b>160–260 mA (~0.8–1.3 W)</b>; Idle: 20–50 mA; <b>Deep Sleep: ~10–15 &mu;A</b></td><td>Idle: <b>~2.7–3.0 W</b>; Full load: <b>8 W to 12 W+</b> (needs <b>5V/5A PSU</b> &amp; active fan cooling)</td></tr></table>`},
{h:'Slide 11 Trap: The Bug in the Arduino Mega Blinking LED Code',b:`<p>Lecture 9 Slide 11 shows a sample sketch intended to blink an LED on the Arduino Mega:</p><pre>void setup() {
  pinMode(6, OUTPUT);
}
void loop() {
  digitalWrite(6, HIGH);
  delay(1000);
  digitalWrite(12, LOW);   // &lt;-- BUG ON SLIDE 11!
  delay(1000);
}</pre><div class="trap"><b>What the Slide Code Actually Does vs How to Fix It:</b><ul><li><b>The Bug:</b> <code>setup()</code> configures <b>pin 6</b> as an <code>OUTPUT</code> and <code>loop()</code> sets <b>pin 6 <code>HIGH</code></b>, waits 1000 ms (1 second), and then calls <code>digitalWrite(12, LOW)</code> on <b>pin 12</b> instead of pin 6!</li><li><b>Visible Effect:</b> Because pin 6 is never set to <code>LOW</code>, <b>the LED on pin 6 turns on and stays on continuously (it never blinks!)</b>. Writing <code>LOW</code> to unconfigured pin 12 has no effect on pin 6.</li><li><b>The Fix:</b> Change <code>digitalWrite(12, LOW);</code> to <b><code>digitalWrite(6, LOW);</code></b> so pin 6 stays on for 1 s and off for 1 s (a 2-second period = 0.5 Hz blink rate).</li></ul></div>`}],
recall:[['Name six board selection criteria.','Processing, memory, power, connectivity, I/O, cost (also size, ecosystem, environment).'],['Best board for edge AI vision?','NVIDIA Jetson.'],['Cheap Wi-Fi sensor node?','ESP32.'],['List the five parameters for choosing a suitable edge development board from Lecture 9 Slide 3.','1) Processing power, 2) Connectivity, 3) GPIO & hardware interfaces, 4) Community & ease of learning, 5) Power consumption.'],['Compare the CPU, clock speed, SRAM, and Flash memory of the Arduino Uno Classic R3 versus the Arduino Uno R4 Minima (Lecture 9 Slide 5).','Classic R3: Microchip ATmega328P (8-bit AVR RISC) @ 16 MHz, 2 KB SRAM, 32 KB Flash. R4 Minima: Renesas RA4M1 (32-bit Arm Cortex-M4 with FPU) @ 48 MHz, 32 KB SRAM, 256 KB Flash.'],['Compare the GPIO pin count, PWM count, Analog input (ADC) count, Hardware UART count, and baseline current draw of the Arduino Uno R3 vs Arduino Mega 2560.','Uno R3: 14 digital I/O (6 PWM), 6 analog inputs, 1 UART, ~45 mA (~0.22 W) at 5V. Mega 2560 (ATmega2560, 8 KB SRAM, 256 KB Flash, 4 KB EEPROM): 54 digital I/O (15 PWM), 16 analog inputs (10-bit ADC), 4 hardware UARTs, ~70 mA (~0.35 W) at 5V.'],['State the CPU, SRAM, ADC/DAC channels, logic voltage tolerance, and deep-sleep current of the ESP32 from Lecture 9 Slides 9–10.','CPU: Tensilica Xtensa Dual-Core 32-bit LX6 @ up to 240 MHz + ULP co-processor. Memory: 520 KB SRAM + 4–16 MB external SPI Flash. Analog: 18 channels of 12-bit ADC and 2 channels of 8-bit DAC. Logic level: 3.3V (NOT 5V tolerant!). Power: 160–260 mA active Wi-Fi TX, 20–50 mA idle, and ~10–15 microamps in Deep Sleep mode.'],['State the processor, RAM options, power requirements, and analog sensor limitation of the Raspberry Pi 5 from Lecture 9 Slides 7–8.','Processor: Broadcom BCM2712 Quad-core 64-bit Arm Cortex-A76 @ 2.4 GHz. RAM: 4 GB, 8 GB, or 16 GB LPDDR4X. Power: ~2.7–3.0 W idle, 8 W to 12 W+ under full load (requires dedicated 5V/5A power supply and active fan cooling). Analog limitation: it has NO onboard ADC, so reading an analog sensor requires an external ADC IC (or a companion MCU).'],['What bug exists in the Arduino Mega LED blink code on Lecture 9 Slide 11, and what happens when the code runs?','The code configures pinMode(6, OUTPUT) and sets digitalWrite(6, HIGH), but then calls digitalWrite(12, LOW) instead of digitalWrite(6, LOW). Because pin 6 is never set LOW, the LED on pin 6 turns on and remains on permanently instead of blinking.']],
quiz:[{k:'mcq',q:'A battery-powered soil sensor sending data every hour over Wi-Fi:',o:['Jetson','ESP32','Desktop PC','Raspberry Pi 5'],a:1},{k:'tf',q:'Raspberry Pi is a microcontroller.',a:false},{q:'Which board from Lecture 9 has NO onboard Analog-to-Digital Converter (ADC) and therefore requires an external ADC integrated circuit to read analog sensors?',o:['Arduino Uno R3 (6 ADC pins)','Arduino Mega 2560 (16 ADC pins)','ESP32 (18 channels of 12-bit ADC)','Raspberry Pi 5 (40-pin digital GPIO header only)'],a:3,e:'Lecture 9 Slide 8 explicitly states that the Raspberry Pi 5 has "No onboard Analog-to-Digital Converter (ADC); requires an external ADC IC for analog reading."',k:'mcq',w:'Lecture 9 Slide 8 explicitly states that the Raspberry Pi 5 has "No onboard Analog-to-Digital Converter (ADC); requires an external ADC IC for analog reading."'},{q:'How many dedicated hardware UART serial ports do the Arduino Uno, ESP32, and Arduino Mega 2560 have respectively (Lecture 9)?',o:['Uno: 1, ESP32: 3, Mega 2560: 4','Uno: 4, ESP32: 1, Mega 2560: 2','Uno: 2, ESP32: 2, Mega 2560: 2','Uno: 0, ESP32: 1, Mega 2560: 1'],a:0,e:'Uno has 1 hardware UART (Slide 5), ESP32 has 3 UARTs (Slide 10), and Mega 2560 has 4 dedicated hardware UART ports (Slide 6).',k:'mcq',w:'Uno has 1 hardware UART (Slide 5), ESP32 has 3 UARTs (Slide 10), and Mega 2560 has 4 dedicated hardware UART ports (Slide 6).'},{q:'Which statement about operating logic voltages on Lecture 9 is TRUE?',o:['All four boards (Uno, Mega, ESP32, Raspberry Pi 5) use 5V logic and are 5V tolerant.','Arduino Uno and Mega operate at 5V logic, whereas ESP32 and Raspberry Pi 5 operate at 3.3V logic (and ESP32 GPIOs are NOT 5V tolerant).','ESP32 operates at 12V logic while Arduino Uno operates at 3.3V logic.','Raspberry Pi 5 requires 24V logic on its 40-pin GPIO header.'],a:1,e:'Uno and Mega use 5V logic; ESP32 and Raspberry Pi 5 use 3.3V logic, and Slide 10 warns that ESP32 GPIOs are "3.3V logic level, not 5V tolerant".',k:'mcq',w:'Uno and Mega use 5V logic; ESP32 and Raspberry Pi 5 use 3.3V logic, and Slide 10 warns that ESP32 GPIOs are "3.3V logic level, not 5V tolerant".'},{q:'What is the visible behaviour of the Lecture 9 Slide 11 sketch where `setup()` has `pinMode(6, OUTPUT);` and `loop()` runs `digitalWrite(6, HIGH); delay(1000); digitalWrite(12, LOW); delay(1000);`?',o:['The LED on pin 6 blinks on and off every 1 second.','The LED on pin 6 turns ON and stays ON continuously because `LOW` is mistakenly written to pin 12 instead of pin 6.','The Arduino Mega fails to compile and throws a syntax error.','Both pin 6 and pin 12 blink alternately at 10 Hz.'],a:1,e:'Because `digitalWrite(12, LOW)` targets pin 12 rather than pin 6, pin 6 remains `HIGH` forever and the LED never turns off.',k:'mcq',w:'Because `digitalWrite(12, LOW)` targets pin 12 rather than pin 6, pin 6 remains `HIGH` forever and the LED never turns off.'},{q:'Which board features a Tensilica Xtensa Dual-Core 32-bit LX6 processor @ 240 MHz, 520 KB SRAM, integrated Wi-Fi + BLE, an Ultra-Low-Power (ULP) co-processor, and a ~10–15 µA Deep Sleep mode?',o:['Arduino Uno R3','Arduino Mega 2560','ESP32','Raspberry Pi 5'],a:2,e:'Slides 9–10 list these exact specifications for the Espressif ESP32.',k:'mcq',w:'Slides 9–10 list these exact specifications for the Espressif ESP32.'}],
theory:[{q:'Recommend boards for (a) a smart-door camera with face recognition and (b) a remote weather station on battery. Justify.',pts:['Criteria stated','(a) Jetson/RPi + reasons','(b) ESP32/Arduino + LoRa + reasons','Power','Connectivity','Cost'],model:`<p>(a) Face recognition needs AI compute and camera support: Jetson Nano (or Raspberry Pi with an accelerator). (b) Battery weather station needs low power and long range: ESP32 or Arduino with LoRa, deep sleep and solar charging.</p>`},{q:'Using the five hardware selection parameters from Lecture 9, compare the Arduino Uno, Arduino Mega 2560, ESP32, and Raspberry Pi 5. Then recommend and justify the optimal board for: (a) a battery-powered wireless soil sensor reporting hourly for a year; (b) a wired CNC/3D-printer controller wiring 45 digital limit/stepper lines, 12 analog thermistors, and 3 UART modules; and (c) a local edge gateway running Linux, an MQTT broker, and dual MIPI camera video analytics. Finally, identify and fix the code bug in Slide 11\'s LED blink sketch.',model:`<p><b>Background:</b> Selecting embedded hardware begins by distinguishing a bare-metal <b>Microcontroller Unit (MCU)</b> from a Linux-capable <b>Single-Board Computer (SBC)</b> to avoid hardware misallocation. Lecture 9 evaluates boards across five parameters: <b>(1) Processing power, (2) Connectivity, (3) GPIO &amp; hardware interfaces, (4) Community &amp; ease of learning, and (5) Power consumption</b>.</p><p><b>Answer and solution:</b></p><p><b>1. Four-Board Comparison Across the 5 Parameters:</b></p><ul><li><b>Arduino Uno (R3 / R4 Minima):</b> 8-bit ATmega328P @ 16 MHz with 2 KB SRAM &amp; 32 KB Flash (R4: 32-bit Cortex-M4 @ 48 MHz, 32 KB SRAM, 256 KB Flash); USB only (no native wireless); 14 digital I/O (6 PWM), 6 ADC inputs, 1 UART/1 SPI/1 I<sup>2</sup>C at 5V logic; ~45 mA (~0.22 W); highest ease of learning.</li><li><b>Arduino Mega 2560:</b> 8-bit ATmega2560 @ 16 MHz, 8 KB SRAM, 256 KB Flash, 4 KB EEPROM; USB only; <b>54 digital I/O (15 PWM), 16 analog inputs (10-bit ADC), 4 hardware UARTs</b> at 5V logic; ~70 mA (~0.35 W); same beginner-friendly Arduino workflow.</li><li><b>ESP32:</b> Dual-core 32-bit Xtensa LX6 @ 240 MHz + ULP co-processor, 520 KB SRAM + 4–16 MB SPI Flash; <b>on-chip Wi-Fi (802.11 b/g/n) + Bluetooth 4.2/BLE</b>; ~34 GPIOs (3.3V logic, not 5V tolerant), <b>18-ch 12-bit ADC, 2-ch 8-bit DAC</b>, capacitive touch, 3 UART/2 SPI/2 I<sup>2</sup>C/2 I<sup>2</sup>S; 160–260 mA active TX but <b>~10–15 &mu;A in Deep Sleep</b>.</li><li><b>Raspberry Pi 5 (SBC):</b> Quad-core 64-bit Arm Cortex-A76 (BCM2712) @ 2.4 GHz, 4/8/16 GB LPDDR4X RAM, PCIe 2.0 NVMe/MicroSD, running full Linux; Dual-band 802.11ac Wi-Fi, BT 5.0/BLE, Gigabit Ethernet (PoE+); 40-pin 3.3V GPIO header, 2&times;4-lane MIPI camera/display, <b>NO onboard ADC</b>; draws 2.7–3.0 W idle and <b>8–12 W+ full load</b> (5V/5A PSU + active cooling).</li></ul><p><b>2. Scenario Recommendations:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a) Battery Wireless Soil Sensor &rarr; ESP32:</b> Needs native Wi-Fi/BLE connectivity, a 12-bit ADC for the soil probe, and a <b>10–15 &mu;A deep-sleep mode</b> so it can wake once an hour, transmit, and sleep for multi-year battery life.</li><li><b>(b) 45-Digital-Pin / 12-Analog / 3-UART Wired Controller &rarr; Arduino Mega 2560:</b> Needs 54 digital I/O pins (&gt; 45), 16 analog ADC inputs (&gt; 12), and 4 hardware UARTs (&ge; 3) with deterministic 5V bare-metal timing and no wireless overhead.</li><li><b>(c) Linux Edge Gateway with MQTT Broker &amp; Dual Cameras &rarr; Raspberry Pi 5:</b> Requires a 64-bit Linux OS, 4–16 GB RAM, Gigabit Ethernet/Wi-Fi, and dual 4-lane MIPI camera transceivers.</li></ul><p><b>3. Slide 11 Blink Sketch Bug &amp; Fix:</b></p><p>In Slide 11's <code>loop()</code>, <code>digitalWrite(6, HIGH)</code> turns pin 6 on, but <code>digitalWrite(12, LOW)</code> writes <code>LOW</code> to <b>pin 12</b> instead of pin 6. As a result, the LED on pin 6 stays on permanently. Fixing <code>digitalWrite(12, LOW);</code> to <b><code>digitalWrite(6, LOW);</code></b> makes the LED blink cleanly (1 s ON, 1 s OFF).</p><p><b>Scenario:</b> In a smart campus farm, 20 solar/battery <b>ESP32</b> soil nodes wake from 15 &mu;A deep sleep every hour to publish moisture readings over Wi-Fi to a mains-powered <b>Raspberry Pi 5</b> edge gateway, while an <b>Arduino Mega 2560</b> inside the greenhouse control panel drives 30 relay valves and reads 12 analog sensors.</p><p><b>How they combine:</b> Each board operates in its sweet spot — the ESP32 and Mega 2560 handle low-power wireless sensing and high-pin-count deterministic I/O at the perception/edge layer, while the Raspberry Pi 5 aggregates their streams and bridges to Azure.</p><p><b>Conclusion:</b> Evaluating processing, connectivity, GPIO/ADC/logic levels, ease of use, and power draw systematically eliminates hardware misallocation across any IoT deployment.</p>`,rubric:['Compares Uno, Mega 2560, ESP32, and Raspberry Pi 5 across all 5 parameters with exact figures (clock, RAM, ADC count, UART count, logic voltage, power draw)','Selects ESP32 for (a), Arduino Mega 2560 for (b), and Raspberry Pi 5 for (c) with parameter-based justifications','Identifies the pin 6 vs pin 12 bug in Slide 11\'s blink sketch, explains that the LED stays permanently ON, and gives the corrected digitalWrite(6, LOW) line'],pts:['Compares Uno, Mega 2560, ESP32, and Raspberry Pi 5 across all 5 parameters with exact figures (clock, RAM, ADC count, UART count, logic voltage, power draw)','Selects ESP32 for (a), Arduino Mega 2560 for (b), and Raspberry Pi 5 for (c) with parameter-based justifications','Identifies the pin 6 vs pin 12 bug in Slide 11\'s blink sketch, explains that the LED stays permanently ON, and gives the corrected digitalWrite(6, LOW) line']}]}
],

};
